/* generated by tools/make-lessons.js from tools/lessons/svm-kernels/*.js; do not edit by hand */
window.LESSONS = Object.assign(window.LESSONS || {}, {"svm-kernels/lift":{"idea":"When no threshold or line splits the classes, add a new coordinate computed from the old one, such as z². In the bigger space a straight line may work, and seen from the original data that line becomes a curved boundary.","why":"Real data is rarely split by one straight cut. Here twelve sensor readings have failing ones at both ends and healthy ones in the middle, so the best single threshold gets only 9 of 12 right (25% wrong). Lifting keeps the margin and support-vector machinery you already know and still lets the boundary bend.","analogy":"A ruler with the good readings near the middle mark and the bad ones near both ends. Any single cut leaves bad readings on both pieces. Now bend the ruler into a U. The ends climb the sides and the middle stays at the bottom, so one flat horizontal cut across the U separates them. Straighten the ruler again and that one cut shows up as two cut marks.","vocab":[["Feature map (lift)","A rule φ that turns each input into a longer list of numbers. Here <span class=\"eq\">φ(z) = (z, z²)</span>: one number in, two numbers out."],["Lifted space","The plane of the new coordinates. The SVM draws its straight line here, not on the original number line."],["Separable","Some straight cut (a threshold on a line, a line in a plane) puts every point of one class on one side and every point of the other class on the other."],["Threshold","One cut point on the number line. Readings above it get one class, readings below it get the other."],["Parabola","The U-shaped curve <span class=\"eq\">y = x²</span>. Every lifted point (z, z²) lies exactly on it."],["Half-width (margin)","The distance from the boundary line to the nearest lifted point, <span class=\"eq\">1/‖w‖</span>. The whole street is twice that, <span class=\"eq\">2/‖w‖</span>, the \"street width\" of the max-margin page. This page shows both."]],"steps":[{"h":"One feature, and no threshold works","p":"Each reading z is one number. Healthy readings (blue) sit near 0. Failing readings (orange) sit at both ends: −2.0, −1.55, −1.25 on the left and 1.2, 1.6, 2.05 on the right. A threshold is one cut that splits the line into a left half and a right half. The best cut still leaves three readings wrong, so the best you can do is 9 of 12."},{"h":"The pattern needs two cuts","p":"Walk along the line: three failing, then six healthy, then three failing. The class changes twice, so you need two cuts. A straight boundary on a one-dimensional line has only one cut. The model has the wrong shape for this data, and no tuning of C will fix a wrong shape."},{"h":"Give each reading a second coordinate","p":"Compute z² for every reading and use the pair (z, z²) as its new address. Healthy readings have z² of at most 0.3025 (the reading −0.55). Failing readings have z² of at least 1.44 (the reading 1.2). The second coordinate now has an empty gap between 0.3025 and 1.44. Nothing was measured again: z² is calculated from z."},{"h":"A straight line now works","p":"Plot the pairs. All lifted points lie on the parabola <span class=\"eq\">y = x²</span>. The middle readings sit at the bottom of the bowl and the end readings climb its sides. A straight line across the bowl puts healthy points below it and failing points above it. Run the ordinary linear SVM of Session 3 on these pairs: same margin, same support vectors, same formulas."},{"h":"Read the line back on the number line","p":"The line is <span class=\"eq\">f(z) = w₁z + w₂z² + b = 0</span>. On the original axis that is a quadratic equation in z, and a quadratic has up to two solutions. So one straight line in the plane becomes two thresholds on the number line. Readings between them are called healthy and readings outside are called failing. A flat boundary in the lifted space is a bent one in the original space."},{"h":"The choice of lift is the choice of shape","p":"Using only z² (a one-dimensional lift) also works here and gives symmetric thresholds, ±0.933. Using (z, z²) adds the freedom to shift them: −0.967 and 0.917. Using z alone is no lift at all, so you are back to one cut. The coordinates you add decide which boundary shapes a straight line can make."},{"h":"A lift is not magic","p":"A line in the (z, z²) plane cuts the parabola in at most two places, so along z it makes at most three blocks: outside, inside, outside. Drag one healthy reading beyond the failing ones on the right and the order becomes failing, healthy, failing, healthy: four blocks, three cuts needed. No line separates that. You would need z³ as well."},{"h":"The price, and what comes next","p":"Here the lift costs one extra number per reading. With 64 pixel values and every product up to degree 3, a lifted point has 47,905 coordinates. The SVM only needs dot products between lifted points, and the next page shows a formula that returns them without building the points. That formula is the kernel."}],"example":{"title":"the twelve readings, solved by hand","setup":"Lift every reading to (z, z²). The SVM leaves four readings exactly on the street edges, where <span class=\"eq\">y·f = 1</span>: z = −1.25 and 1.2 (failing, y = +1) and z = −0.55 and 0.5 (healthy, y = −1). Three of them are enough to find w₁, w₂ and b.","table":{"head":["Reading z","Label y","Lifted point (z, z²)","f(z) = (80z² + 4z − 71) / 49","y · f","Role"],"rows":[["−1.25","+1","(−1.25, 1.5625)","+1.00","1.00","on an edge"],["1.20","+1","(1.2, 1.44)","+1.00","1.00","on an edge"],["−0.55","−1","(−0.55, 0.3025)","−1.00","1.00","on an edge"],["0.50","−1","(0.5, 0.25)","−1.00","1.00","on an edge"],["0.05","−1","(0.05, 0.0025)","−1.44","1.44","inside its own side"],["−2.00","+1","(−2, 4)","+4.92","4.92","far outside"]]},"walk":["Thresholds first. Sorted along the line the classes read F F F H H H H H H F F F (F = failing, H = healthy). Any single cut leaves at least three readings wrong, so the best is <b>9 of 12</b>.","Edge equations. Put <span class=\"eq\">f(z) = w₁z + w₂z² + b</span> equal to y on three edge readings:<br>(1) z = −1.25: −1.25·w₁ + 1.5625·w₂ + b = +1<br>(2) z = 1.2: 1.2·w₁ + 1.44·w₂ + b = +1<br>(3) z = 0.5: 0.5·w₁ + 0.25·w₂ + b = −1","Subtract (2) from (1). The b cancels: −2.45·w₁ + 0.1225·w₂ = 0, so <b>w₁ = 0.05·w₂</b>.","Subtract (3) from (2): 0.7·w₁ + 1.19·w₂ = 2. Put in w₁ = 0.05·w₂: (0.035 + 1.19)·w₂ = 2, so <b>w₂ = 2 / 1.225 = 1.6327</b> (that is 80/49) and <b>w₁ = 0.0816</b> (4/49).","Back into (3): b = −1 − 0.5 × 0.0816 − 0.25 × 1.6327 = <b>−1.449</b> (−71/49).","Check the fourth edge reading, z = −0.55: f = 0.0816 × (−0.55) + 1.6327 × 0.3025 − 1.449 = −1.000. Check a reading that is not on an edge, z = 0.05: f = −1.44, so y·f = 1.44, more than 1, safely outside the street.","Half-width: ‖w‖ = √(0.0816² + 1.6327²) = 1.6347, so the half-width (the margin) is 1/‖w‖ = <b>0.6117</b> and the whole street is 2/‖w‖ = <b>1.2235</b> wide.","Thresholds: f = 0 means 80z² + 4z − 71 = 0. The quadratic formula gives z = (−4 ± √22736) / 160 = (−4 ± 150.78) / 160, so <b>z = −0.967 and z = 0.917</b>.","Use it. z = 0.9 gives f = (64.8 + 3.6 − 71)/49 = −0.053: healthy. z = 1.0 gives f = (80 + 4 − 71)/49 = 0.265: failing. The boundary sits between them, at 0.917."],"result":"<span class=\"eq\">w = (4/49, 80/49) ≈ (0.082, 1.633)</span>, <span class=\"eq\">b = −71/49 ≈ −1.449</span>, half-width 0.612, thresholds −0.967 and 0.917, four readings on the street edges. The widget shows the same numbers (half-width 0.6117, street width 1.2235). One straight line in the lifted plane did the work of two thresholds."},"read":[["Blue circles and orange triangles on the top number line","Healthy and failing readings. Drag one sideways to move it; every number on the page is recomputed."],["Dotted vertical line (top)","The best single threshold. Wherever you put one cut, at least three readings stay wrong."],["Solid dark-blue vertical lines with numbers (top)","The thresholds implied by the SVM in the lifted space: the places where f(z) = 0. With the (z, z²) map there are two."],["Black rings around readings","The readings touching a street edge. Drawn only when the lifted data are separable."],["Dashed curve in the bottom plot","The parabola y = z². Every lifted point sits on it."],["Black solid line in the bottom plot","The SVM boundary f = 0 in the lifted plane. The dashed orange and blue lines are the street edges, f = +1 and f = −1."],["Slider: Lift amount (picture only)","Slides the second coordinate from 0 up to z². The SVM always uses the full lift."],["Readout: Best single threshold on the line","How many of the 12 readings the best one-cut rule gets right."],["Readout: Separable after the lift?","\"yes\" when every lifted point is on its correct side and outside the street (the page fits with C = 10,000). Otherwise \"no: the lifted points overlap, so no line is shown\", and the fit readouts and lines are hidden."],["Readouts: w and b","w = (weight on z, weight on z²). The score is f(z) = w₁z + w₂z² + b, as in the worked example."],["Readouts: Half-width 1/‖w‖ and Street width 2/‖w‖","The distance from the line to the nearest lifted point, and the whole street, which is twice that."],["Readouts: Thresholds on the original line, Support vectors","The roots of f(z) = 0 on the number line, and the z values of the readings that touch the street edges."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Press <i>Animate the lift</i> (or drag <i>Lift amount (picture only)</i> from 0 to 1).","see":"The readings rise from the floor onto the dashed parabola. The six middle readings barely move. The outer ones climb to heights between 1.44 and 4.2. At full lift one straight line splits the two colours.","why":"The height is z², which is tiny near 0 and grows fast. The slider squashes the whole picture, line included, so separability never changes on the way: the SVM always uses the full lift."},{"do":"Set <i>Map</i> to <i>z² only</i>.","see":"The bottom plot becomes a number line of z² values with a single cut. The readouts show thresholds −0.933 and 0.933, two support vectors (z = −0.55 and 1.20), a half-width of about 0.569 and a street width of about 1.138.","why":"One coordinate, z², already separates this data. The nearest healthy and failing values are 0.3025 and 1.44. The cut sits halfway, at 0.8713, and z = ±√0.8713 = ±0.933. The margin is half the gap: (1.44 − 0.3025) / 2 = 0.569."},{"do":"Set <i>Map</i> to <i>z only (no lift)</i>.","see":"\"Separable after the lift?\" says no, every fit readout (w, b, half-width, street width, thresholds, support vectors) shows a dash, the bottom plot draws no line, and the best single threshold is still 9 of 12.","why":"With the identity map you are back on the number line, and one cut cannot isolate a block in the middle."},{"do":"Press <i>Reset readings</i>. Drag the failing reading at −1.25 into the middle of the blue group, to about z = 0.","see":"\"Separable after the lift?\" changes to \"no: the lifted points overlap\", the black rings and the lines vanish, and the fit readouts show dashes.","why":"The lifted points all lie on a parabola and a line cuts a parabola in at most two places, so the classes can form at most three blocks along z. Now the order is F F H H H F H H H F F F: five blocks."},{"do":"Press <i>Reset readings</i>. Drag the healthy reading at 0.5 to the right, to about 0.9.","see":"The data stay separable, but the half-width falls from 0.612 to about 0.32 and the thresholds move outward to about −1.11 and 1.06. The moved reading becomes a support vector.","why":"The nearest healthy and failing points set the street. Moving a healthy point from z² = 0.25 up to 0.81 brings it close to the failing group (z² = 1.44), so the street narrows."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Bend the ruler into a U. The ends climb, the middle stays down, and one flat cut separates them. Straighten the ruler and the cut becomes two marks: z = −0.967 and 0.917."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Count the colour changes along the line. Each change needs one threshold, and a line in the (z, z², …, zᵈ) space gives at most d thresholds. Two changes need d = 2. Four colour blocks (three changes) need d = 3."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"After lifting, look for an empty gap in the new coordinate. Healthy z² ≤ 0.3025 and failing z² ≥ 1.44 leave a gap, so a line must exist. For a fitted line, every reading on a street edge must give y·f = 1 exactly."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Score = w₁·z + w₂·z² + b. The sign is the class, the zeros of the score are the thresholds, and |score| = 1 marks the street edges."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"Whatever you do to the training data you must do to new data. Build <code>np.column_stack([z, z**2])</code> before every <code>predict</code> or <code>decision_function</code>."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The SVM draws a curve through the readings.\"","right":"It draws a <b>straight</b> line in the lifted plane. The curve (here, a pair of thresholds) only appears when you read that line back on the original axis."},{"wrong":"\"Lifting adds information about the sensor.\"","right":"No new measurement is taken: z² is calculated from z. A lift only changes which boundary shapes a straight line can make."},{"wrong":"\"After a lift, any data become separable.\"","right":"Only if the new coordinates can express the shape. The (z, z²) lift allows at most two thresholds, so four colour blocks along z still overlap in the lifted plane."},{"wrong":"\"The half-width readout is the width of the street.\"","right":"Half-width is 1/‖w‖ = 0.612, the distance from the line to the nearest point. The street is twice that, 2/‖w‖ = 1.22, and the page shows it as Street width."}],"code":[{"title":"Count what the best single threshold can do","py":"import numpy as np\n\nz = np.array([-0.55, -0.35, -0.15, 0.05, 0.25, 0.5, -2.0, -1.55, -1.25, 1.2, 1.6, 2.05])\ny = np.array([0] * 6 + [1] * 6)                # 0 = healthy, 1 = failing\n\ncuts = (np.sort(z)[:-1] + np.sort(z)[1:]) / 2   # one cut halfway between each pair of neighbours\nbest = 0\nfor t in cuts:\n    says_failing = (z > t).astype(int)          # rule A: \"failing if z is above the cut\"\n    best = max(best, (says_failing == y).sum(), (1 - says_failing == y).sum())   # rule B: the reverse\nprint(\"best single threshold:\", best, \"of\", len(z))","out":"best single threshold: 9 of 12","walk":[["y = np.array([0] * 6 + [1] * 6)","The labels in the same order as <code>z</code>: six healthy (0) then six failing (1). <code>[0] * 6</code> repeats the list six times and <code>+</code> joins the two lists."],["(np.sort(z)[:-1] + np.sort(z)[1:]) / 2","Sort the readings, then average each neighbouring pair. <code>[:-1]</code> drops the last value and <code>[1:]</code> drops the first, so the two arrays line up as (left neighbour, right neighbour). Cutting halfway between neighbours is enough: any other position gives the same split."],["(z > t).astype(int)","Rule A says \"failing\" for every reading above the cut. The comparison gives True or False; <code>astype(int)</code> turns that into 1 or 0 so it can be compared with <code>y</code>."],["(1 - says_failing == y).sum()","Rule B is the opposite rule, because <code>1 - says_failing</code> flips every 0 and 1. A threshold can point either way, so try both. <code>(a == y).sum()</code> counts how many readings match their label."],["best = max(best, ...)","Keep the highest count seen over every cut and both rules."]],"tip":"No cut and no rule beats 9 of 12. That is the evidence that you need more than a threshold on z."},{"title":"Lift the readings and fit a linear SVM","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nz = np.array([-0.55, -0.35, -0.15, 0.05, 0.25, 0.5, -2.0, -1.55, -1.25, 1.2, 1.6, 2.05])\ny = np.array([-1] * 6 + [1] * 6)               # -1 = healthy, +1 = failing\n\nZ = np.column_stack([z, z**2])                 # the lift: each reading z becomes the row (z, z squared)\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1e6, tol=1e-9).fit(Z, y)\n(w1, w2), b = svm.coef_[0], svm.intercept_[0]\nprint(\"w =\", np.round([w1, w2], 4), \" b =\", round(b, 4), \" accuracy:\", svm.score(Z, y))\nprint(\"half-width:\", round(1 / np.hypot(w1, w2), 4), \" on the edge:\", np.sort(z[np.isclose(y * svm.decision_function(Z), 1, atol=1e-6)]))\nprint(\"thresholds on the line:\", np.round(np.sort(np.roots([w2, w1, b])), 3))","out":"w = [0.0816 1.6327]  b = -1.449  accuracy: 1.0\nhalf-width: 0.6117  on the edge: [-1.25 -0.55  0.5   1.2 ]\nthresholds on the line: [-0.967  0.917]","walk":[["np.column_stack([z, z**2])","Builds the lifted table: one row per reading, column 0 is z and column 1 is z². <code>z**2</code> squares every entry at once. The shape is (12, 2), which is what <code>fit</code> expects: rows are samples, columns are features."],["SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1e6, tol=1e-9)","The ordinary linear SVM. A huge <code>C</code> allows almost no mistakes, like the hard margin. A tight <code>tol</code> makes the solver stop only when the answer is precise, so the weights match the exact 4/49 and 80/49."],["(w1, w2), b = svm.coef_[0], svm.intercept_[0]","<code>coef_</code> has one row per boundary, hence <code>[0]</code>. Unpacking splits it into the weight on z and the weight on z². Attributes that end in an underscore exist only after <code>fit</code>."],["y * svm.decision_function(Z)","Each reading's score f times its label y. Exactly 1 means the reading touches a street edge, so <code>np.isclose(..., 1, atol=1e-6)</code> picks out the edge readings."],["np.roots([w2, w1, b])","Solves w₂·z² + w₁·z + b = 0: the thresholds on the original line. The list holds the coefficients, highest power first."]],"tip":"svm.support_ may list only three of the four edge readings: a reading exactly on an edge can carry weight 0 when three others already fix w₁, w₂ and b. The widget rings all four."},{"title":"Score new readings: lift them the same way","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nz = np.array([-0.55, -0.35, -0.15, 0.05, 0.25, 0.5, -2.0, -1.55, -1.25, 1.2, 1.6, 2.05])\ny = np.array([-1] * 6 + [1] * 6)\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"linear\", C=1e6, tol=1e-9).fit(np.column_stack([z, z**2]), y)\n\nnew = np.array([0.9, 1.0, -1.5])               # three new readings\nlifted = np.column_stack([new, new**2])        # the SAME lift as in training\nprint(\"score f:\", np.round(svm.decision_function(lifted), 3))\nprint(\"class  :\", svm.predict(lifted))","out":"score f: [-0.053  0.265  2.102]\nclass  : [-1  1  1]","walk":[["new = np.array([0.9, 1.0, -1.5])","Three readings as a flat list. They are still single numbers, one feature each."],["np.column_stack([new, new**2])","The model was trained on two columns, so every new reading needs two columns too. Passing <code>new.reshape(-1, 1)</code> would raise an error about the number of features."],["svm.decision_function(lifted)","The score f for each row. 0.9 gives −0.053 (just healthy) and 1.0 gives 0.265 (just failing): the boundary sits between them, at 0.917."],["svm.predict(lifted)","The sign of f turned back into the labels −1 and +1."]],"tip":"The hand calculation f(1.0) = (80 + 4 − 71)/49 = 0.265 matches the code. If your numbers disagree, check that you lifted the new data."}],"check":[{"q":"Why does adding the coordinate z² let a <i>straight</i> line separate these readings?","options":["Squaring moves every reading further from every other reading","Healthy readings have small z² and failing readings have large z², so one flat cut between them splits the classes","The SVM gets more training points once z² is added","Squaring turns the failing readings into healthy ones"],"answer":1,"why":"Healthy readings have z² of at most 0.3025 and failing ones of at least 1.44, so a line at about z² = 0.87 separates them. Squaring adds no points and changes no labels."},{"q":"Using f(z) = (80z² + 4z − 71) / 49, what does the fitted SVM say about a new reading z = 1.0?","options":["Healthy, f ≈ −1.45","Healthy, f ≈ −0.27","Failing, f ≈ 0.27","Failing, f ≈ 1.7"],"answer":2,"why":"f(1.0) = (80 + 4 − 71) / 49 = 13/49 = 0.265. It is positive, so failing, and small, so close to the threshold at 0.917."},{"q":"You drag the healthy reading at 0.5 out to z = 2.5, beyond all the failing readings, and keep the (z, z²) map. What does \"Separable after the lift?\" show?","options":["yes, squaring always keeps the classes apart","no, but only because 2.5 is outside the plotted range","yes, if C is made larger","no: along z the classes now form four blocks, and a line in the (z, z²) plane gives at most three"],"answer":3,"why":"The order along z is F F F H H H H H F F F H: four blocks need three cuts, and a line cuts the parabola in at most two places. A larger C cannot create a line that does not exist."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why adding z² helps when no single threshold separates the readings, and what the straight line looks like back on the number line.","model":"The failing readings are far from zero at both ends, so their squares are large while the healthy readings have small squares; with that second coordinate a straight line can split them. Back on the original number line, that one line is two thresholds, at about −0.97 and 0.92, with the healthy readings between them."},"cheat":"Lift: add computed coordinates, here z → (z, z²), so a straight line in the new space separates what no cut could on the original axis. Fit an ordinary linear SVM there: f = w₁z + w₂z² + b. On the original line f = 0 is a quadratic: two thresholds (−0.967 and 0.917). Half-width (margin) = 1/‖w‖ = 0.612, street = 2/‖w‖. Always lift new data the same way."},"svm-kernels/kernel-identities":{"idea":"A kernel K(a, b) returns the dot product of two lifted points straight from a and b, so an SVM can work in a huge, even infinite, lifted space without ever building it.","why":"Lifting 64 pixel values with every product up to degree 3 gives each image 47,905 coordinates. Building and multiplying those for thousands of images is slow and uses huge memory. The SVM only ever needs dot products, so a formula that returns the dot product directly turns that work into a 64-term sum.","analogy":"Multiplying 99 × 101. The long way: write both numbers out, multiply digit by digit, add the rows. The shortcut: 100² − 1 = 9999. Same answer, almost no work. The explicit lift is the long multiplication. The kernel is the shortcut formula. The SVM only ever asks for the answer (the dot product), never for the working (the lifted points).","vocab":[["Dot product","<span class=\"eq\">a·b</span>: multiply matching coordinates and add. One number that is large when the two points point the same way."],["Lift φ","The rule that turns a point into a longer list of coordinates, as on the previous page."],["Kernel K(a, b)","A function with <span class=\"eq\">K(a, b) = φ(a)·φ(b)</span> for some lift φ. You evaluate it from a and b alone."],["Polynomial kernel","<span class=\"eq\">(γ·a·b + r)<sup>d</sup></span>. d is the degree and r the offset. In scikit-learn: <code>degree</code>, <code>coef0</code> and <code>gamma</code>."],["Gram matrix","The table of <span class=\"eq\">K(xᵢ, xⱼ)</span> for every pair of training points. It is all the SVM ever sees of the data."],["α·y (dual weights)","One weight per training point. The score is <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = Σ αᵢyᵢ K(xᵢ, x) + b</span>. A point with weight 0 does not appear in it."],["RBF kernel","<span class=\"eq\">exp(−γ‖a − b‖²)</span>: 1 for identical points and falling towards 0 with distance."],["Taylor series","Writing a function as a sum of powers. Here <span class=\"eq\">eˣ = Σ xⁿ / n!</span> for n = 0, 1, 2, …"]],"steps":[{"h":"The SVM touches the data only through dot products","p":"In Session 3 the trained score was <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = Σ αᵢyᵢ (xᵢ·x) + b</span>, and the training problem also used the data only through <span class=\"eq\">xᵢ·xⱼ</span>. The coordinates themselves never appeared on their own. This one fact is what makes the kernel trick possible."},{"h":"After a lift, they become dot products of lifted points","p":"With a lift φ, every <span class=\"eq\">xᵢ·xⱼ</span> becomes <span class=\"eq\">φ(xᵢ)·φ(xⱼ)</span> and nothing else changes: same margin, same support vectors, same formulas. The long way is to build both lifted points and multiply them. If φ has 47,905 coordinates, that is a lot of work for one number."},{"h":"Find the lift hiding inside a formula","p":"Expand the square: <span class=\"eq\">(ab + r)² = 2r·ab + a²b² + r²</span>. Each term is a product of one coordinate of a and the matching one of b: <span class=\"eq\">(√(2r)·a)(√(2r)·b) + a²·b² + r·r</span>. So the lift is <span class=\"eq\">φ(z) = (√(2r)·z, z², r)</span>. For a = 2, b = 3, r = 1 both routes give 49."},{"h":"The shortcut: the kernel","p":"Define <span class=\"eq\">K(a, b) = φ(a)·φ(b)</span>. In this example K is just <span class=\"eq\">(ab + r)²</span>: one multiply, one add, one square. For two 64-pixel images the degree-3 kernel needs one 64-term dot product, not two vectors of 47,905 numbers. The kernel answers the dot-product question without building φ."},{"h":"Swap it in everywhere","p":"Train on the Gram matrix of values <span class=\"eq\">K(xᵢ, xⱼ)</span> in place of the dot products, and score with <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = Σ αᵢyᵢ K(xᵢ, x) + b</span>. The boundary is flat in the lifted space and curved in the original one. The cost moves from the size of φ to the size of the table: n × n entries."},{"h":"XOR: no line, but a kernel works","p":"XOR is four points at the corners of a square, with the same colour on opposite corners. No line splits them: a linear SVM gets 2 of 4. With <span class=\"eq\">K = (x·z + 1)²</span> the SVM gives each point weight α = 1/8 and b = 0, and the score is exactly <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = x₁x₂</span>: positive when the signs of x₁ and x₂ agree, negative when they differ."},{"h":"RBF: every degree at once","p":"Split the exponent: <span class=\"eq\">e^(−γ(a−b)²) = e^(−γa²) · e^(−γb²) · e^(2γab)</span>. The last factor has the Taylor series <span class=\"eq\">Σ (2γab)ⁿ / n!</span>, and each term is a polynomial kernel of degree n, divided by n!. Degrees 1 and 2 together give a lift like the (z, z²) of the previous page, up to scaling. Adding every degree gives infinitely many coordinates."},{"h":"Why infinity is affordable","p":"The weights 1/n! shrink faster than powers grow, so a few terms already match the exact value to many digits. But you never need the series: one RBF value costs one subtraction, one square and one exponential. The real prices are still the n × n table and the risk of memorising, which the next pages measure."}],"example":{"title":"XOR by hand with K = (x·z + 1)²","setup":"Four points: (1, 1) and (−1, −1) with y = +1, and (1, −1) and (−1, 1) with y = −1. Kernel <span class=\"eq\">K(x, z) = (x·z + 1)²</span>. Find the SVM weights α, the offset b and the score.","table":{"head":["K(row, column)","(1, 1)","(−1, −1)","(1, −1)","(−1, 1)"],"rows":[["(1, 1)","9","1","1","1"],["(−1, −1)","1","9","1","1"],["(1, −1)","1","1","9","1"],["(−1, 1)","1","1","1","9"]]},"walk":["Fill the Gram table. The diagonal is (x·x + 1)² = (2 + 1)² = 9. For (1, 1) and (−1, −1): x·z = −2, so (−2 + 1)² = 1. For (1, 1) and (1, −1): x·z = 0, so (0 + 1)² = 1. Every off-diagonal entry is 1.","The four corners are mirror images of each other, so by symmetry the four weights are equal: αᵢ = a.","The dual objective is Σα − ½ΣΣ αᵢαⱼ yᵢyⱼ Kᵢⱼ. Add up yᵢyⱼKᵢⱼ over the table: 4 × 9 on the diagonal, plus 4 × (+1) for the two same-colour pairs (both orders), minus 8 × 1 for the four opposite-colour pairs (both orders). Total 36 + 4 − 8 = <b>32</b>.","So the objective is 4a − ½·32·a² = 4a − 16a². Its maximum is where 4 − 32a = 0, so <b>a = 1/8</b>. The constraint Σαy = 0 holds because two weights are positive and two negative.","The score is f(x) = ⅛[(x₁ + x₂ + 1)² + (−x₁ − x₂ + 1)² − (x₁ − x₂ + 1)² − (−x₁ + x₂ + 1)²] + b. Let s = x₁ + x₂ and d = x₁ − x₂. The first pair is 2 + 2s² and the second pair is 2 + 2d², so the bracket is 2(s² − d²) = 8x₁x₂ and <b>f(x) = x₁x₂ + b</b>.","Find b from a point on a street edge. At (1, 1) the score must be +1: ⅛(9 + 1 − 1 − 1) + b = 1 + b = 1, so <b>b = 0</b>.","Test a new point, (1.2, −0.7): f = 1.2 × (−0.7) = −0.84, negative, so class −1. The four corners give f = +1, +1, −1, −1 exactly."],"result":"α = 1/8 for all four points, b = 0, f(x) = x₁x₂. The boundary f = 0 is the two axes (a cross) and the street edges f = ±1 are the curves x₁x₂ = ±1 through the corners. The widget prints the same weights (0.125, 0.125, −0.125, −0.125) and scores 1, 1, −1, −1."},"read":[["Panel 1 table: Kernel, computed directly","The value (a·b + r)² from one small formula."],["Panel 1 table: Lifted points φ(z)","The three coordinates (√(2r)·z, z², r) of each point. They are built only to prove the number is right. The kernel never needs them."],["Panel 1 table: Dot product and Difference","The long route. The difference is 0 or about 1e-15 (rounding), because both routes are the same algebra."],["Panel 2 table: Gram matrix","The 4 × 4 table of (x·z + r)² between the four corners. At r = 1 it is 9 on the diagonal and 1 elsewhere."],["Panel 2: SVM weights α·y and b","The fitted dual weights, in the order (1,1), (−1,−1), (1,−1), (−1,1), and the offset b. A line under them says what to expect: ±1/8 for every r above 0, and only two supports at r = 0."],["Panel 2: f at the four points","The score at each corner. 1, 1, −1, −1 means every corner sits exactly on a street edge."],["Panel 2 plot: lines and tint","Solid black is f = 0. Dashed orange and blue are f = +1 and f = −1. The tint shows the predicted class."],["Panel 3 bars","The contribution of degree n: e^(−γa²−γb²)·(2γab)ⁿ / n!. Blue if positive, orange if negative (when ab is negative the bars alternate). Faded bars are beyond \"Terms kept\"."],["Panel 3 black line with dots","The running total after each degree. The dashed orange horizontal line is the exact kernel value; the black line climbs to it."],["Panel 3 table: Error after n terms","|exact − running total|. Hover over a bar to read its own term and the running total."],["Slider: Terms kept (degrees 0 … n−1)","How many terms of the series are used. The maximum is 14."]],"experiments":[{"do":"In panel 1 press <i>a = 2, b = 3, r = 1</i>, then <i>r = ½</i>. Then set a = −1.7, b = 3.3 and r = 0.35 with the sliders.","see":"The kernel and the dot product agree each time: 49 and 49, then 42.25 and 42.25, then 27.6676 and 27.6676. The Difference row is 0 or about 1e-15.","why":"The kernel is the dot product after algebra: (ab + r)² = 2r·ab + a²b² + r² is the sum of three coordinate products. Only rounding in the last digit can separate the two routes."},{"do":"Slide <i>offset r</i> in panel 1 down to 0 (a = 2, b = 3).","see":"The lifted points become (0, 4, 0) and (0, 9, 0), and the kernel is 36 = (2·3)², the pure square term.","why":"With r = 0 the terms 2r·ab and r² vanish and only a²b² is left. The offset r decides how much of the lower-order parts (the straight-line term and the constant) the kernel keeps."},{"do":"In panel 2 move <i>Kernel offset r</i> through 0.5, 1, 1.5 and 2.","see":"The Gram matrix changes (its diagonal is 6.25, 9, 12.25, 16), but α·y stays at 0.125, 0.125, −0.125, −0.125, b stays 0 and f stays at 1, 1, −1, −1.","why":"Add up yᵢyⱼKᵢⱼ and every r-dependent part cancels between same-colour and opposite-colour pairs. The total is 32 for every r, so the dual problem 4a − 16a² always has the answer a = 1/8. For XOR the boundary does not depend on r."},{"do":"Move the same slider to 0.","see":"The weights become 0, 0.25, 0, −0.25: only two support vectors. The scores are still 1, 1, −1, −1.","why":"With r = 0 the kernel is (x·z)², and (1, 1) and (−1, −1) land on the same lifted point (x₁², √2·x₁x₂, x₂²) = (1, 1.41, 1). So do the two orange corners. The weight can sit on either twin, and the solver put all of it on one point of each pair."},{"do":"In panel 3 (defaults a = 1, b = −0.4, γ = 0.5) drag <i>Terms kept</i> from 1 up to 10.","see":"The running total goes 0.5599, 0.3359, 0.3807, 0.3748, 0.3754 and so on to the exact 0.3753111. After 10 terms the error is 1.6e-11.","why":"Because ab is negative the terms alternate in sign, and they shrink like 1/n!. Each new degree corrects the previous overshoot by a smaller amount."},{"do":"Raise γ in panel 3 to 1, 2 and 3 (keep a = 1, b = −0.4) and count the terms until the error is below 1e-6. Then set a = b = 2 with γ = 1.","see":"You need 7, 9, 11 and 13 terms for γ = 0.5, 1, 2 and 3. With a = b = 2 and γ = 1 even 14 terms leave an error of about 3e-2.","why":"The terms are (2γab)ⁿ / n!. They grow until n is about 2γ|ab| and only then shrink. A larger γ or |ab| moves that peak to the right (n = 7 and 8 for a = b = 2, γ = 1), so more terms are needed."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"K(a, b) is the dot product of the lifted points. For (ab + r)² the lift is (√(2r)·z, z², r). The 2 in √(2r) is what produces the cross term 2r·ab."},{"tag":"Picture","text":"The Gram matrix is a table of how alike each pair of points is. Everything the SVM knows about your data is in that table. Row i, column j: how alike points i and j look in the lifted space."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"To trust a kernel claim, compute both routes for one pair. K(2, 3) at r = 1: kernel (6 + 1)² = 49, lifted 12 + 36 + 1 = 49."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"XOR needs the product x₁x₂ as a coordinate. A degree-2 polynomial kernel contains it, which is why f = x₁x₂ appears."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"Count the lifted coordinates for p inputs and degree d: C(p + d, d). That is 6 for p = 2, d = 2 and 47,905 for p = 64, d = 3. The kernel needs only p multiplications either way."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"scikit-learn's polynomial kernel is <code>(gamma·x·z + coef0) ** degree</code>. With <code>gamma=1, coef0=r, degree=2</code> it is exactly the kernel of this page."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The kernel trick builds the lifted points and then multiplies them.\"","right":"It never builds them. K(2, 3) = 49 comes from (2·3 + 1)². The lifted points are drawn only to show why that number is right."},{"wrong":"\"RBF has infinitely many coordinates, so it must be slow or must overfit.\"","right":"One RBF value costs one exponential. How much it overfits is set by γ and C, not by the number of coordinates."},{"wrong":"\"The constant third coordinate r in the lift changes the classifier.\"","right":"It is the same for every point, so it never moves a point. It adds r² to every kernel value, and that cancels because Σαy = 0. (r matters through the 2r·ab term.)"},{"wrong":"\"An SVM cannot solve XOR.\"","right":"A <i>linear</i> SVM scores 2 of 4. With K = (x·z + 1)² the same SVM fits all four corners exactly."}],"code":[{"title":"Check that the kernel equals the dot product of lifted points","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.metrics.pairwise import polynomial_kernel\n\na, b = 2.0, 3.0\nfor r in (1.0, 0.5):\n    phi = lambda z: np.array([np.sqrt(2 * r) * z, z**2, r])      # the lift hiding inside (a*b + r)**2\n    lifted = phi(a) @ phi(b)                                       # the long way: lift, then dot product\n    direct = polynomial_kernel([[a]], [[b]], degree=2, gamma=1, coef0=r)[0, 0]   # the shortcut\n    print(f\"r={r}: lifted {lifted:.2f}, kernel {direct:.2f}\")","out":"r=1.0: lifted 49.00, kernel 49.00\nr=0.5: lifted 42.25, kernel 42.25","walk":[["lambda z: np.array([...])","A one-line function. It returns the three lifted coordinates <code>(√(2r)·z, z², r)</code> of a reading z. <code>np.sqrt(2 * r)</code> is √(2r)."],["phi(a) @ phi(b)","The <code>@</code> operator is the dot product of two arrays: multiply matching entries and add. This is the expensive route."],["polynomial_kernel([[a]], [[b]], ...)","scikit-learn's kernel function. It wants 2-D tables (rows are points), hence the double brackets, and returns a table of all pairs; <code>[0, 0]</code> picks the only entry."],["degree=2, gamma=1, coef0=r","The kernel is <code>(gamma · a·b + coef0) ** degree</code>. With gamma 1 and degree 2 this is (ab + r)²."],["f\"r={r}: lifted {lifted:.2f}\"","An f-string. <code>{lifted:.2f}</code> prints the value with two decimals, so tiny rounding differences do not show."]],"tip":"Change a, b and r. The two numbers always agree, because they are the same algebra written two ways."},{"title":"Solve XOR with a polynomial kernel","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX = np.array([[1, 1], [-1, -1], [1, -1], [-1, 1]])       # the four XOR corners\ny = np.array([1, 1, -1, -1])\nprint(\"linear accuracy:\", SVC(kernel=\"linear\").fit(X, y).score(X, y))\n\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"poly\", degree=2, gamma=1, coef0=1, C=1e6, tol=1e-10).fit(X, y)   # K = (x.z + 1)**2\nprint(\"alpha*y:\", np.round(svm.dual_coef_[0], 4), \" b is zero:\", bool(abs(svm.intercept_[0]) < 1e-6))\ntest = np.array([[1.2, -0.7], [0.5, 0.5]])               # two new points\nprint(\"f:\", np.round(svm.decision_function(test), 3), \" x1*x2:\", test[:, 0] * test[:, 1])","out":"linear accuracy: 0.5\nalpha*y: [-0.125 -0.125  0.125  0.125]  b is zero: True\nf: [-0.84  0.25]  x1*x2: [-0.84  0.25]","walk":[["SVC(kernel=\"linear\")","A straight boundary. On XOR it gets exactly 2 of 4 right (0.5), no better than guessing."],["kernel=\"poly\", degree=2, gamma=1, coef0=1","The polynomial kernel <code>(gamma · x·z + coef0) ** degree</code> = (x·z + 1)². <code>C=1e6</code> and a tiny <code>tol</code> make it behave like a hard margin and give the exact weights."],["svm.dual_coef_[0]","The product α·y for each support vector. It lists support vectors by class (−1 first), not in your input order, so the signs read −, −, +, +."],["svm.intercept_[0]","The offset b. We print whether it is (numerically) zero rather than the number, so a tiny −0.0 does not appear."],["test[:, 0] * test[:, 1]","<code>test[:, 0]</code> is the first column (x₁) and <code>test[:, 1]</code> the second (x₂). Their product is x₁x₂, which should equal the SVM score."]],"tip":"The score equals x1*x2 at points the SVM never saw, not only at the four corners: the formula is exact, not a fit."},{"title":"Add up the RBF series term by term","py":"import math\nimport numpy as np\n\na, b, g = 1.0, -0.4, 0.5\nprefix = np.exp(-g * a**2 - g * b**2)            # e^(-g a^2) * e^(-g b^2): does not depend on n\ntotal = 0.0\nfor n in range(6):                               # degrees 0 ... 5\n    term = prefix * (2 * g * a * b) ** n / math.factorial(n)   # polynomial kernel of degree n, divided by n!\n    total += term\n    print(n, f\"{term:+.6f}\", f\"{total:.6f}\")\nprint(\"exact:\", f\"{np.exp(-g * (a - b) ** 2):.6f}\")","out":"0 +0.559898 0.559898\n1 -0.223959 0.335939\n2 +0.044792 0.380731\n3 -0.005972 0.374759\n4 +0.000597 0.375356\n5 -0.000048 0.375308\nexact: 0.375311","walk":[["prefix = np.exp(-g * a**2 - g * b**2)","The two outer factors e^(−γa²)·e^(−γb²) from splitting the exponent. They are the same for every term."],["(2 * g * a * b) ** n / math.factorial(n)","The n-th term of the Taylor series of e^(2γab): the polynomial kernel (2γ·ab)ⁿ divided by n!. <code>**</code> is \"to the power of\"."],["total += term","Running total after adding degree n. This is the black line in the widget's third panel."],["f\"{term:+.6f}\"","<code>+</code> always prints the sign and <code>.6f</code> keeps six decimals. The terms alternate in sign because ab = −0.4 is negative."],["np.exp(-g * (a - b) ** 2)","The exact RBF kernel value. Six terms already agree with it to 5 decimals (0.375308 against 0.375311)."]],"tip":"Change g to 3 and print more terms: they stay large for longer before shrinking."}],"check":[{"q":"Why can an SVM use <span class=\"eq\">(a·b + 1)²</span> without ever computing the lifted points?","options":["The kernel approximates the lifted points with fewer coordinates","Training and scoring use the data only through dot products, and the kernel returns the dot product of the lifted points directly","The lifted points are stored on disk and loaded when needed","The SVM drops the lifted coordinates that it does not need"],"answer":1,"why":"The dual problem and the score use only xᵢ·xⱼ. Replace it by K(xᵢ, xⱼ) = φ(xᵢ)·φ(xⱼ) and the answer is identical to the one from lifting, with no lifted point built. Nothing is dropped or approximated."},{"q":"What is K(a, b) = (ab + r)² for a = 1, b = 4 and r = 2?","options":["36","20","6","25"],"answer":0,"why":"K = (1·4 + 2)² = 6² = 36. Check by lifting: φ(1) = (√4·1, 1, 2) = (2, 1, 2) and φ(4) = (8, 16, 2), and the dot product is 16 + 16 + 4 = 36. The value 6 forgets the square, 20 = 4² + 2² forgets the cross term 2r·ab, and 25 uses r = 1."},{"q":"In the XOR panel you move the offset r from 1 to 1.5. What happens to the weights α·y, to b and to the scores at the four corners?","options":["b becomes non-zero","The data become inseparable","Nothing: still ±0.125, b = 0 and scores 1, 1, −1, −1","The weights grow because the Gram matrix entries grow"],"answer":2,"why":"The sum of yᵢyⱼKᵢⱼ is 32 for every r, because the r-dependent parts cancel. The dual 4a − 16a² keeps its maximum at a = 1/8, so the weights, b and the scores f = x₁x₂ do not change. Only the Gram matrix itself changes."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend why an SVM does not need to build the lifted points, and what the RBF kernel adds up to.","model":"The SVM uses the data only through dot products, and a kernel such as (a·b + r)² gives the dot product of the lifted points straight from a and b, so the lift is never built. The RBF kernel is the same idea taken to the limit: polynomial kernels of every degree added with weights 1/n!, an infinite lift that still costs one exponential per pair."},"cheat":"Kernel: K(a, b) = φ(a)·φ(b), computed from a and b without building φ. The SVM needs only dot products, so it trains on the Gram matrix K(xᵢ, xⱼ) and scores f(x) = Σ αᵢyᵢ K(xᵢ, x) + b. (ab + r)² lifts to (√(2r)·a, a², r); K(2, 3) = 49 at r = 1. XOR: α = 1/8, f = x₁x₂. RBF = every degree n of (2γab)ⁿ/n!."},"svm-kernels/playground":{"idea":"The kernel decides which boundary shapes are possible, and C and γ decide how flexible the boundary is. Comparing training accuracy with cross-validated accuracy tells you whether it is too stiff (underfit) or too wiggly (overfit).","why":"A boundary that looks right on the points you trained on can be wrong on new ones. On the moons, γ = 1000 gets 100% on the training points and 53% on held-out points: no better than a coin. Reading the picture and two accuracies catches this before the model is used.","analogy":"A cartographer draws the border between two kingdoms. With a ruler (linear kernel) the border is a straight line. With a French curve (polynomial) it bends smoothly. With a freehand pen (RBF) it can follow anything, even a small circle round every village (overfit). A ruler through a winding coast is underfit. To judge a border, check it against villages the cartographer never surveyed: that is cross-validation.","vocab":[["Decision boundary","Where the score f(x) = 0. Points on one side are predicted class A, points on the other side class B."],["Underfit","The boundary is too stiff for the data. Training and cross-validated accuracy are both low and close together, and a more flexible setting does better."],["Overfit","The boundary chases individual points. Training accuracy is high and cross-validated accuracy is much lower."],["CV accuracy","Split the points into 5 groups. Train on 4, score on the 5th, repeat five times and average. An estimate of accuracy on new points."],["Gap","Training accuracy minus CV accuracy. A gap of 10 points or more is the overfitting warning."],["Polynomial kernel","<span class=\"eq\">(γ·x·z + r)<sup>d</sup></span>. Degree d is how bendy the boundary can be. Offset r keeps the lower-order terms. With r = 0 only the degree-d terms remain."],["Vote","<span class=\"eq\">α·y·K(support vector, probe)</span>: one support vector's contribution to the score at a spot. The score is the sum of the votes plus b."],["Support-vector share","Support vectors divided by the number of points. It is high when γ is far too small or far too large."]],"steps":[{"h":"The kernel chooses the family of shapes","p":"The boundary is where <span class=\"eq\">f(x) = Σ αᵢyᵢK(xᵢ, x) + b</span> equals 0. The kernel K decides which shapes are possible. Linear: a straight line. Polynomial: smooth curves up to degree d. RBF: any shape built from local bumps. A shape that cannot match the data stays wrong whatever C you choose."},{"h":"What C and γ do","p":"C is the price of a mistake. A small C accepts mistakes for a smoother boundary. A large C bends the boundary to catch every training point. γ is the reach of each point (it also scales the polynomial kernel). A small γ gives a smooth, global boundary. A large γ gives local islands. Start from γ = \"scale\" and C = 1, then turn one dial at a time."},{"h":"Reading the picture","p":"The tint shows the predicted class everywhere. The solid line is f = 0. The dashed lines are f = ±1, the street. Rings mark support vectors, the points that touch or break the street. A smooth boundary with a clear street and few rings is a calm fit. Islands, wiggles and rings on nearly every point are a nervous one."},{"h":"Two accuracies and the gap","p":"Training accuracy asks how well the model recalls points it saw. 5-fold CV asks how it does on points it did not see. The gap between them is your overfitting meter. With 80 points one point is 1.25 percentage points, so a gap or a difference of one or two points is noise."},{"h":"Underfit looks like this","p":"Training and CV are both low and close together, and the boundary is too stiff for the shape: a line through moons or rings. A more flexible setting does noticeably better. On the moons a linear kernel scores 88% and 89%, while RBF with γ = 1 scores 100% and 100%. Low is only \"underfit\" if something else does better."},{"h":"Overfit looks like this","p":"Training accuracy is high, often 100%, and CV is far lower: a gap of 10 points or more. The boundary breaks into islands round single points and nearly every point is a support vector. On the moons, γ = 1000 gives training 100%, CV 53% and 80 of 80 support vectors. A very large C can overfit too."},{"h":"Neither: the noise ceiling","p":"On the Overlap data the classes really do overlap. CV peaks at about 81% (RBF with C = 10, or a degree-2 polynomial) and no setting does much better. Pushing flexibility further only raises training accuracy: C = 1000 gives 90% on training and 65% in CV. When nothing lifts CV, you have hit the limit of the data, so stop and keep the simplest model."},{"h":"A routine for diagnosing","p":"Set γ = \"scale\", C = 1. Read training and CV. If both are low, add flexibility (a bendier kernel, a larger γ or C). If the gap is 10 points or more, remove flexibility (a smaller γ or C). Move dials by factors of 10, one at a time. Use the probe to see who votes in an empty spot: if the offset b decides there, γ is too large."}],"example":{"title":"diagnosing six settings on the moons","setup":"Start from: Two moons, first sample (before pressing New sample): 80 points, 40 per class. RBF with C = 1 in the five RBF rows. Training accuracy, 5-fold CV and the support-vector count are read off the Readouts panel.","table":{"head":["Setting","Training","5-fold CV","Gap","Support vectors","Diagnosis"],"rows":[["Linear","88%","89%","−1","25 of 80","underfit: a line on curved data"],["RBF, γ = 0.01","85%","85%","0","46 of 80","underfit: bumps wider than the picture"],["RBF, γ = “scale” (0.22)","96%","95%","+1","27 of 80","decent; can improve"],["RBF, γ = 1","100%","100%","0","27 of 80","good fit"],["RBF, γ = 100","100%","63%","+37","80 of 80","overfit: memorises"],["RBF, γ = 1000","100%","53%","+47","80 of 80","overfit: chance on new points"]]},"walk":["Compute the gap, training minus CV, for each row: −1, 0, +1, 0, +37, +47. Only the last two rows exceed 10 points.","A small gap does not mean a good model. The first two rows also have gaps near 0 but low scores. Look at the level too: another setting (γ = 1) reaches 100%, so 88% and 85% are not a ceiling. Those rows are underfit. If nothing had passed 89%, you could not call 88% underfit.","Support-vector share is SVs ÷ 80: 31%, 58%, 34%, 34%, 100%, 100%. It is high at both extremes of γ (too wide: many points break the street; too narrow: every point stands alone) and about a third in the good rows.","One CV point is worth 1/80 = 1.25 points. A difference of 100% against 98% is one or two points, which is noise. (RBF with γ = 10 gives about 100% and 98% with about 70 supports: the count jumping from 27 to about 70 is the real sign that it is tipping towards memorising.)","γ = \"scale\" is 1 / (n_features × Var(X)) where Var(X) is the variance over every entry. Hand example: points (0,0), (2,0), (0,2), (2,2). The eight entries 0,0,2,0,0,2,2,2 have mean 1 and variance 1, so scale = 1 / (2 × 1) = 0.5. For the moons it is 0.2214, a factor of 4.5 below the best γ here.","Verdict by rule: gap of 10 points or more means overfit. Both well below the best CV you can find means underfit. The highest CV with a small gap is the fit to keep."],"result":"Keep RBF with γ around 1 (100% and 100%, 27 supports). Linear and γ = 0.01 are underfit. γ = 100 and 1000 are overfit: gaps of 37 and 47 points and every point a support vector. The default γ = \"scale\" is decent but a factor of 4.5 too small for these moons."},"read":[["Blue circles and orange triangles","Class A and class B points. A click adds a point of the colour chosen under \"Click on the plot to…\", removes one, or places the probe. Dragging any point always moves it."],["Tinted regions","The predicted class at every spot: blue for class A, orange for class B. A darker tint means a larger |f|, so the model is surer."],["Solid black curve","The decision boundary, f = 0."],["Dashed blue and orange curves","The street edges f = −1 and f = +1. A wide street means a smooth fit."],["Black rings","The support vectors: the points that touch or break the street."],["Star and coloured circles (probe mode)","The star is the probe. A circle around a support vector shows its vote at the star: bigger means a bigger vote, blue for class A, orange for class B."],["Panel: Vote inspector","For a probe: the sum of the votes, the offset b, the score f and the class. For RBF also the effective voters and a warning when b outweighs the votes. Then a table of the six biggest votes."],["Readout: Support vectors","How many of the points are support vectors, out of all points."],["Readout: Training accuracy","The share of the plotted points the model classifies correctly."],["Readout: 5-fold cross-validated accuracy","The CV estimate, with fixed folds. It is recomputed when you release a dragged point or change a setting. While you drag, the old value stays, marked \"(old: updates when you let go)\"."],["Readout: γ = “scale” would be","1 / (n_features × variance of all entries) for the current points. The button γ = “scale” copies it into the γ slider."],["Controls: Kernel, C, γ, Degree, Offset r","C and γ are log sliders: equal slider distances are equal ratios. γ is hidden for Linear. Degree and Offset r appear only for Polynomial. Choosing another Start from data set resets γ to \"scale\" for that data."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Choose <i>Start from</i>: XOR (γ resets to “scale”, 0.351), then RBF. Read the three readouts, then switch to <i>Polynomial</i> (degree 3, r = 1) and to <i>Linear</i>.","see":"RBF: 17 of 48 support vectors, training 100%, CV 98%. Polynomial: 8 supports, 100% and 98%. Linear: 38 supports, 65% and 65%.","why":"XOR needs the product x₁x₂, which a straight line cannot make. Linear has a small gap and low scores: textbook underfit. The polynomial kernel contains the product and fits with only 8 supports."},{"do":"Choose <i>Two moons</i>, RBF, C = 1. Move γ to 0.01, then about 1, about 10 and 1000.","see":"Support vectors: 46, about 27, about 70 and 80 of 80. CV: 85%, about 100%, about 98% and 53%. Training rises from 85% to 100%.","why":"The support-vector count is high at both ends: very small γ makes bumps wider than the picture, so many points break the street. Very large γ makes every point its own island. The count is lowest where the fit is best."},{"do":"Set γ = 1000, choose <i>probe</i> under \"Click on the plot to…\", and click between the two moons.","see":"The Vote inspector shows votes summing to about 0, b = 0.022, and \"The offset outweighs the vote here: b decides.\" The empty plane is faintly orange because b is positive. At the default γ (“scale”) a click at the centre shows votes summing to about −0.3, ten times larger than b = 0.026.","why":"At σ = 0.022 no support vector is close enough to speak, so every vote vanishes and the sign of b labels the whole empty plane."},{"do":"Choose <i>Overlapping groups</i> (γ resets to “scale”, 0.52), keep RBF, and move C from about 1 up to 1000.","see":"Training accuracy rises from about 77% to 90% while 5-fold CV falls from about 79% to 65%. Support vectors fall from about 34 to 26 of 48.","why":"A huge C punishes every training mistake so hard that the boundary twists round a few noisy points. Training improves, new-point accuracy gets worse. Note that the support-vector count went down while it overfitted, so judge by the gap, not by the count."},{"do":"Choose <i>Two moons</i> (first sample), <i>Polynomial</i>, set Degree to 2 and Offset r to 1, then slide r all the way to 0.","see":"At r = 1: training 89%, CV 89%, 28 supports. At r = 0: training 51%, CV 48%, 78 supports. Setting Degree back to 3 with r = 0 recovers to 86% and 88%.","why":"With r = 0 and degree 2 the score is an even function, f(−x) = f(x). The two moons are mirror images through their centre with the colours swapped, which needs the opposite sign. r > 0 adds odd terms. With r = 0 and degree 3 the score is odd, f(−x) = −f(x), which is exactly what swapped colours need."},{"do":"Choose <i>Two separated groups</i> (γ resets to “scale”) and compare the three kernels.","see":"All three score 100% and 100%. Supports out of 28: Linear 4, Polynomial 3, RBF 8.","why":"When the groups are well separated, extra flexibility buys nothing. Prefer the simplest model that reaches the best CV."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Ruler, French curve, freehand pen: the kernel picks the tool. A pen that circles every village is overfit and a ruler through a coastline is underfit."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Training minus CV: below about 3 points is fine, 10 or more is overfit. Both low and equal means underfit, but only if something else scores higher. CV is the number to maximise."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Start at γ = \"scale\", C = 1. Move one dial at a time and by factors of 10, so each move is big enough to see."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"Probe an empty spot between your classes. If the inspector says \"b decides\" there, γ is far too large: the model has no opinion in the gaps."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Support-vector share is high at both extremes of γ, and about a third in the good rows of the moons. But a large C can overfit with fewer supports, so use the gap, not the count."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"γ_scale = 1 / (number of features × variance of all entries). Polynomial kernel: (γ·x·z + r) raised to d."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>svm.score(X, y)</code> is training accuracy, <code>cross_val_score(svm, X, y, cv=5).mean()</code> is the CV readout and <code>svm.n_support_.sum()</code> is the support-vector count."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"A higher training accuracy means a better model.\"","right":"On the moons γ = 1000 gets 100% training accuracy and 53% in CV. Training accuracy only measures recall."},{"wrong":"\"More support vectors always means overfitting, and fewer is always better.\"","right":"γ = 0.01 is underfit with 46 of 80 supports. A large C overfits the Overlap data with fewer supports (34 down to 26). Use the training-minus-CV gap."},{"wrong":"\"RBF is always the best kernel.\"","right":"On well-separated groups all three reach 100%, and the linear kernel needs only 4 supports against 8 for RBF. Choose by CV and prefer the simpler model when scores tie."},{"wrong":"\"The offset r in the polynomial kernel is just a constant and does not matter.\"","right":"On the moons with degree 2, CV is 89% for r = 1 and 48% for r = 0. It decides whether the lower-order terms exist at all."},{"wrong":"\"The colour far away from the points tells you what the model learned.\"","right":"Where no support vector is near, the tint comes from the offset b (RBF) or from the polynomial's growth. At γ = 1000 the empty plane is simply the sign of b."}],"code":[{"title":"Training accuracy, CV accuracy and the gap","py":"from sklearn.datasets import make_moons\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX, y = make_moons(n_samples=80, noise=0.2, random_state=3)\nfor gamma in (0.01, 1, 1000):\n    svm = SVC(kernel=\"rbf\", C=1, gamma=gamma)\n    train = svm.fit(X, y).score(X, y)                       # accuracy on points the model has seen\n    cv = cross_val_score(svm, X, y, cv=5).mean()            # accuracy on points held out in 5 folds\n    print(f\"gamma={gamma:<5} train={train:.2f}  cv={cv:.2f}  gap={train - cv:+.2f}  SVs={svm.n_support_.sum()}/80\")","out":"gamma=0.01  train=0.80  cv=0.79  gap=+0.01  SVs=74/80\ngamma=1     train=0.94  cv=0.93  gap=+0.01  SVs=29/80\ngamma=1000  train=1.00  cv=0.61  gap=+0.39  SVs=80/80","walk":[["make_moons(n_samples=80, noise=0.2, random_state=3)","Two interleaved half-circles with 40 points each. <code>noise</code> is the spread added to each coordinate; <code>random_state</code> fixes the sample so the numbers repeat."],["svm.fit(X, y).score(X, y)","<code>fit</code> returns the model, so <code>.score</code> can follow at once. Scoring on the same X, y gives <b>training</b> accuracy: recall, not prediction."],["cross_val_score(svm, X, y, cv=5).mean()","Clones the model, trains it on 4 folds and scores it on the 5th, five times. It returns five accuracies, and <code>.mean()</code> averages them. The model <code>svm</code> itself is not changed."],["svm.n_support_.sum()","<code>n_support_</code> counts support vectors per class (two numbers); <code>.sum()</code> gives the total."],["{train - cv:+.2f}","Formatting: <code>+.2f</code> shows two decimals and always prints the sign. This is the gap."]],"tip":"The numbers differ from the page because this sample is not the page's sample, but the pattern is the same: a gap of 0.39 at γ = 1000."},{"title":"Which kernel can draw the shape?","py":"from sklearn.datasets import make_moons\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX, y = make_moons(n_samples=80, noise=0.2, random_state=3)\nX = X - X.mean(axis=0)                                      # centre the moons, as the playground does\nmodels = {\"linear\": SVC(kernel=\"linear\"),\n          \"poly d=2 r=0\": SVC(kernel=\"poly\", degree=2, coef0=0, gamma=\"scale\"),\n          \"poly d=3 r=1\": SVC(kernel=\"poly\", degree=3, coef0=1, gamma=\"scale\"),\n          \"rbf\": SVC(kernel=\"rbf\", gamma=\"scale\")}\nfor name, svm in models.items():\n    print(f\"{name:<13} train={svm.fit(X, y).score(X, y):.2f}  cv={cross_val_score(svm, X, y, cv=5).mean():.2f}\")","out":"linear        train=0.88  cv=0.88\npoly d=2 r=0  train=0.51  cv=0.47\npoly d=3 r=1  train=0.95  cv=0.94\nrbf           train=0.94  cv=0.93","walk":[["X - X.mean(axis=0)","Subtracts the mean of each column, so the cloud is centred on (0, 0). Without this the degree-2, r = 0 kernel result would differ, because that kernel treats the origin as special."],["models = {\"linear\": SVC(...), ...}","A dictionary: name on the left, ready-made model on the right. It lets one loop try several kernels."],["coef0=0","This is the offset r of the page. <code>coef0=0</code> with <code>degree=2</code> keeps only degree-2 terms, an even function that cannot fit these moons."],["gamma=\"scale\"","scikit-learn's default: 1 / (n_features × X.var()), the same as the page's button."],["models.items()","Gives each (name, model) pair in turn, so the loop variables <code>name</code> and <code>svm</code> take them one by one."]],"tip":"Poly with r = 0 sits at chance (0.51, 0.47) and r = 1 jumps to 0.94: one parameter, 47 points of CV accuracy."},{"title":"Far from the data the offset b decides","py":"from sklearn.datasets import make_moons\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX, y = make_moons(n_samples=80, noise=0.2, random_state=3)\nspot, far = [[0.5, 0.25]], [[8.0, 8.0]]                     # a gap between the moons, and a point far from all data\nfor gamma in (1, 1000):\n    svm = SVC(kernel=\"rbf\", C=1, gamma=gamma).fit(X, y)\n    print(f\"gamma={gamma:<5} b={svm.intercept_[0]:+.3f}  f(gap)={svm.decision_function(spot)[0]:+.3f}  f(far)={svm.decision_function(far)[0]:+.3f}\")","out":"gamma=1     b=-0.019  f(gap)=-0.033  f(far)=-0.019\ngamma=1000  b=-0.028  f(gap)=-0.028  f(far)=-0.028","walk":[["spot, far = [[0.5, 0.25]], [[8.0, 8.0]]","Two query points, each a table with one row and two columns (hence the double brackets). The first lies between the moons, the second far outside."],["svm.intercept_[0]","The offset b."],["svm.decision_function(spot)[0]","The score f at the spot: the sum of the votes plus b. <code>[0]</code> takes the number out of the one-item result."],["f(far) = b","At γ = 1 the far point has f exactly equal to b because every K is 0 there. At γ = 1000 even the gap between the moons has f = b."]],"tip":"If f at a point equals b to three decimals, no support vector is voting there: the model is guessing."}],"check":[{"q":"A model shows 80 of 80 support vectors, training accuracy 100% and 5-fold CV 55%. What is the problem, and which dial do you turn?","options":["Overfitting (memorising): lower γ, or lower C","The data are noise-limited, so nothing can be done","Nothing: a perfect training score is the goal","Underfitting: raise γ"],"answer":0,"why":"A 45-point gap and every point a support vector is the memorising pattern. Lowering γ widens the bumps (and a lower C reduces how hard each mistake is punished), so the boundary stops circling single points."},{"q":"Four points (0, 0), (4, 0), (0, 4), (4, 4) are the whole data set. What is γ = \"scale\"?","options":["0.25","0.5","4","0.125"],"answer":3,"why":"The eight entries are 0, 0, 4, 0, 0, 4, 4, 4: mean 2, and every entry is 2 away from the mean, so the variance is 4. γ = 1 / (2 features × 4) = 0.125. The value 0.25 forgets the number of features, and 4 is the variance itself."},{"q":"On the Overlap data with γ = \"scale\" you move C from 1 up to 1000. What do the readouts do?","options":["Nothing changes, because C only matters for the linear kernel","Both fall","Training accuracy rises (about 77% to 90%) while CV falls (about 79% to 65%)","Both training and CV accuracy rise"],"answer":2,"why":"A very large C makes every training mistake very costly, so the boundary twists round noisy points. Recall improves and prediction gets worse: a growing gap is overfitting, even though the support-vector count falls."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend how you tell from the page's readouts whether a kernel SVM is underfitting or overfitting.","model":"Compare the training accuracy with the 5-fold cross-validated accuracy: if both are low and close together, and a more flexible setting does better, it is underfitting; if training is near 100% and CV is 10 or more points lower, it is overfitting. Boundaries made of small islands and nearly every point a support vector confirm overfitting, and you fix it by lowering γ or C."},"cheat":"The kernel sets the shape family: line, polynomial curve of degree d (use r > 0), or RBF local bumps (width from γ). Diagnose with training minus CV accuracy. Both low, and a better setting exists: underfit, add flexibility. Gap of 10 points or more: overfit, lower γ or C. Start at γ = \"scale\" = 1/(n_features·Var(X)) and C = 1, then turn one dial at a time."},"svm-kernels/rbf-vote":{"idea":"An RBF SVM scores a new point as a vote of its support vectors. Each one adds a bump of height α·y that fades with distance. The bumps and an offset b add up, and the sign of the total is the prediction.","why":"The formula f(x) = Σ αᵢyᵢ K(xᵢ, x) + b looks abstract until you see it as bumps on a line. The picture explains what γ and C do, why only a few points matter at any spot, and why a huge γ goes wrong: far from every support vector nobody votes and b alone decides.","analogy":"A village decides on loans by asking elders who each remember one past case: repaid or defaulted. Each elder lives at a fixed spot. Their voice carries only so far (that is γ: larger γ, shorter reach) and only so loudly (that is C, the cap on loudness). An applicant hears mostly the nearest elders. If nobody lives close enough, nobody is heard, and the village falls back on its standing habit: the offset b.","vocab":[["Bump","One support vector's vote as a function of position: <span class=\"eq\">αᵢyᵢ · exp(−γ(z − zᵢ)²)</span>. Tallest at zᵢ, fading towards 0 on both sides."],["Support vector","A training point with α > 0. Only these points have a bump at all."],["α·y","The signed height of a bump: positive for failing readings (y = +1), negative for healthy ones (y = −1). Its size can never exceed C."],["Offset b","A constant added to the sum of the bumps. Wherever every bump is 0, the score equals b."],["Probe z*","The new reading you test. The page lists every support vector's vote at z*."],["Effective voters","<span class=\"eq\">(ΣK)² / ΣK²</span>: how many support vectors really count at the probe. It is 1 when one voter dominates and k when all k count equally."],["Width σ","How far a bump reaches: <span class=\"eq\">σ = 1/√(2γ)</span>. γ = 1 gives 0.71, γ = 100 gives 0.071, γ = 0.01 gives 7.1."]],"steps":[{"h":"Write the score as a sum","p":"After training, the RBF SVM scores a point z with <span class=\"eq\">f(z) = Σᵢ αᵢyᵢ · K(zᵢ, z) + b</span>, summed over the support vectors only. Each term is one support vector's vote. The sign of f is the class: positive means failing (+1), negative means healthy (−1)."},{"h":"The weight is a similarity","p":"<span class=\"eq\">K(zᵢ, z) = exp(−γ(z − zᵢ)²)</span> is 1 when z sits on the support vector and falls as z moves away. At γ = 1: distance 0.5 gives 0.78, distance 1 gives 0.37, distance 2 gives 0.018. A support vector shouts at its neighbours and whispers at strangers."},{"h":"One bump per support vector","p":"Plot one vote against z and you get a bell-shaped bump centred on the support vector, with height <span class=\"eq\">αᵢyᵢ</span>. Failing supports (y = +1) give bumps that point up. Healthy supports (y = −1) give bumps that point down. Each thin curve on the page is one bump."},{"h":"Add the bumps, add b","p":"The thick curve is the sum of all bumps plus the offset b. Where up-bumps win it is above zero (failing). Where down-bumps win it is below zero (healthy). The decision boundary is where the thick curve crosses zero."},{"h":"Width is γ, height is C","p":"Width: <span class=\"eq\">σ = 1/√(2γ)</span>. Height: training chooses each αᵢ, but never above C. On these readings the largest height the SVM wants is 1.97, so every C above that gives the same model. C = 0.5 chops every bump to at most 0.5."},{"h":"Who votes at a probe","p":"At a probe z*, only supports within about σ of it have a K worth counting. Effective voters, (ΣK)² / ΣK², counts them. With γ = 1 and z* = 1.0 it is 2.2 of 4. With γ = 100 it is 1.0 of 12: the single nearest reading is the whole vote."},{"h":"Far from everyone, the offset decides","p":"Far from every support vector all K are near 0, so all votes are near 0 and the score is about b. At γ = 1, f(3) = 0.40 while b = 0.32: almost all of the score is b. Where the data give no vote, b answers for them."},{"h":"Not a nearest-neighbour vote","p":"A nearest-neighbour vote divides by the total weight, so the weights add to 1 and even a distant point picks a winner. The SVM does not divide: its votes just fade. At a large γ every point is far from every other, the votes vanish, and b alone labels the gaps (at γ = 1000 b is about 0, so the score is about 0 and no class is named). That is how a huge γ memorises the readings and says little about new ones."}],"example":{"title":"the vote at z* = 1.0, by hand","setup":"The twelve readings with γ = 1 and C = 10. The SVM keeps four support vectors with α·y = +1.969 (failing, z = 1.2 and −1.25) and −1.969 (healthy, z = 0.5 and −0.55), and offset b = 0.3245 (the page shows 0.324). Test the reading z* = 1.0.","table":{"head":["Support vector z","α·y","Distance² (1.0 − z)²","K = exp(−1 · distance²)","Vote α·y·K"],"rows":[["1.2","+1.969","0.04","0.9608","+1.8918"],["0.5","−1.969","0.25","0.7788","−1.5335"],["−0.55","−1.969","2.4025","0.0905","−0.1782"],["−1.25","+1.969","5.0625","0.0063","+0.0125"],["Sum of votes","","","","+0.1926"],["Offset b","","","","+0.3245"],["Score f(1.0)","","","","+0.5171 → failing"]]},"walk":["Distances from z* = 1.0 to the four supports: 0.2, 0.5, 1.55 and 2.25. Square them (third column): 0.04, 0.25, 2.4025, 5.0625.","K = exp(−γ·d²) with γ = 1: e^(−0.04) = 0.9608, e^(−0.25) = 0.7788, e^(−2.4025) = 0.0905, e^(−5.0625) = 0.0063. The two nearest supports have K near 1. The farthest has almost none.","Votes = α·y × K: +1.969 × 0.9608 = +1.8918, −1.969 × 0.7788 = −1.5335, −1.969 × 0.0905 = −0.1782, and +1.969 × 0.0063 = +0.0125 (a calculator with the unrounded K gives 0.0125).","Sum of votes: 1.8918 − 1.5335 − 0.1782 + 0.0125 = 0.1926. The two big votes nearly cancel: a failing support 0.2 to the right of the probe and a healthy one 0.5 to its left. Add the offset: f = 0.1926 + 0.3245 = <b>0.5171</b>. Positive, so failing.","Effective voters: ΣK = 1.8364 and ΣK² = 1.5378, so (1.8364)² / 1.5378 = <b>2.19</b>. About two of the four supports decide.","Check the model itself. A support vector on a street edge must score exactly +1. At z = 1.2: f = 1.969 × (1 − 0.6126 − 0.0468 + 0.0025) + 0.3245 = 1.969 × 0.3431 + 0.3245 ≈ 1.000. It works.","Now move the probe to z* = 3.0, beyond all the data. K is 0.0392 for the support at 1.2 and 0.0019 for the one at 0.5 (the others round to 0.0000). Votes: +0.0771 and −0.0038, sum 0.0733. f = 0.0733 + 0.3245 = 0.398, still failing, but 82% of that score is b."],"result":"f(1.0) = +0.517, failing, decided by the two supports within 0.5 of the probe (2.19 effective voters of 4). At z* = 3 the votes shrink to 0.073 and the offset 0.324 supplies most of f = 0.398. These are the same numbers as the widget's vote table and readouts."},"read":[["Thick black curve","The score f(z): all bumps added, plus b. Above 0 means failing, below 0 means healthy."],["Thin orange curves","Bumps of failing support vectors (α·y > 0). They point up."],["Thin blue curves","Bumps of healthy support vectors (α·y < 0). They point down."],["Dotted grey line labelled b = …","The offset. The thick curve returns to this height wherever the bumps die out."],["Dark-blue dashed vertical line","The probe z*. Drag it or use the Probe slider."],["Dots on the probe line","Each support vector's vote at z*: a triangle for a positive vote, a circle for a negative one. The black diamond is the total score f(z*)."],["Strip labelled data","The twelve readings (blue circles healthy, orange triangles failing) with black rings on the support vectors. Drag a reading sideways to move it."],["Table: Votes at the probe","One row per support vector, biggest vote first: α·y, K = exp(−γ(z* − z)²) and the vote α·y·K. Below them the sum of votes, b and the score f(z*)."],["Readout: Support vectors","How many of the 12 readings have a bump."],["Readout: Score at probe, f(z*)","The total, and the class it implies. A score below 1e-9 in size reads \"about 0: no class named\"."],["Readout: Effective voters at probe","(ΣK)² / ΣK² and the number of support vectors it is counted out of."],["Readout: Positions where |votes| < |b|","The share of the axis from −3 to 3 where the bumps add up to less than the offset, so b effectively decides. Flagged as a warning above 30%. It reads \"n/a\" when b is below 1e-9, because then the score is about 0 there, not decided by b."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Leave the defaults (γ = 1, C = 10, probe 1.00) and read the table <i>Votes at the probe</i>.","see":"Four rows: +1.89 (z = 1.2), −1.53 (0.5), −0.18 (−0.55) and +0.01 (−1.25). The votes sum to 0.19, b is 0.324 and f is 0.517, so failing. Effective voters: 2.2 of 4.","why":"The two nearest supports (0.2 and 0.5 away) carry the vote and nearly cancel. The farther ones barely speak."},{"do":"Drag the probe line (or the slider) to the far right, to about z* = 3.","see":"The votes shrink to +0.08 and −0.004 (the others are 0.000). f falls to about 0.40, close to b = 0.32, and effective voters drop to about 1.1.","why":"K = exp(−γd²) dies quickly: at distance 1.8 from the support at 1.2 it is only 0.04. With no voters left, f approaches b."},{"do":"Raise γ to 100, then to 1000, keeping C = 10.","see":"Support vectors rise to 12 of 12 and the bumps become thin spikes at every reading. Effective voters at the probe fall to 1.0 of 12, and b shrinks to about 0.012 at γ = 100 and 0.000 at γ = 1000. At γ = 1000 the score at the probe reads \"about 0: no class named\" and \"Positions where…\" reads n/a.","why":"σ is 0.071 and 0.022, narrower than the 0.2 gaps between neighbouring readings. So every reading stands alone as a spike of height about 1, and between spikes the score is just b. At γ = 1000, b is about 0, so the gaps get no real information at all."},{"do":"Set γ back to 1. Lower C to about 0.5, then to 0.1, then raise it to 1000.","see":"At C ≈ 0.5, 11 of 12 readings are support vectors and no bump is taller than 0.5. At C = 0.1 all 12 are supports with bumps 0.1 tall. From C = 2 up to 1000 the model is identical: the same 4 supports at ±1.97.","why":"C caps the bump height. Once C is above the height the data actually need (1.97), the cap is never reached, so a larger C changes nothing."},{"do":"Set γ = 0.01 (the left end of the slider) with C = 10.","see":"All 12 readings are support vectors. The thick curve is a shallow wave that never leaves about ±0.4, and four readings end up on the wrong side: the failing ones at −2, −1.55, −1.25 and 1.2.","why":"σ is 7.1, wider than the whole axis. Bumps of height 10 (the cap) overlap and cancel, and their sum cannot make the dip-and-rise pattern. A bump that is too wide fails as surely as one that is too narrow."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"Elders at fixed houses, voices that fade with distance, and a village habit b when nobody is close enough to be heard."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"Score = Σ (signed height × closeness) + b. Signed height is α·y (never above C in size). Closeness is exp(−γ × distance²)."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Compare the width σ = 1/√(2γ) with the spacing of your data. σ much larger than the spacing: too smooth. σ much smaller: every point stands alone. Here readings are 0.2 or more apart, so γ = 1 (σ = 0.71) works and γ = 100 (σ = 0.07) memorises."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"A support vector on a street edge must score exactly ±1. If your hand calculation does not give ±1 at a support vector (here f(1.2) = +1), you have an arithmetic slip or a wrong weight."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"Effective voters tells you how local the vote is. On these readings at the probe 1.0 it is 12.0 at γ = 0.01, 2.2 at γ = 1 and 1.0 at γ = 100."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>dual_coef_</code> holds α·y, <code>support_vectors_</code> the bump centres and <code>intercept_</code> the offset b. Rebuild the score by hand from them to check you have read them correctly."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The RBF SVM is just a nearest-neighbour classifier.\"","right":"Only roughly. A nearest-neighbour vote is normalised. The SVM votes are not: they fade, and where they all fade, b decides. At γ = 1000 the votes vanish and b is about 0, so the score in the gaps is about 0 and the page names no class."},{"wrong":"\"Every training point votes.\"","right":"Only support vectors do: 4 of 12 at γ = 1. At γ = 1000 all 12 are supports, but each one is audible only within about 0.02 of itself."},{"wrong":"\"A bigger C always gives a different, stronger model.\"","right":"C only caps the bump heights. On these readings any C from 2 to 1000 gives the same model, because the cap (1.97 needed) is never touched."},{"wrong":"\"A larger γ fits better, because training accuracy rises.\"","right":"γ = 100 and 1000 classify all 12 readings correctly, but only because each reading has its own spike. Between spikes the score is just b: 0.012 at γ = 100 and 0.000 at γ = 1000, which tells you nothing."}],"code":[{"title":"Fit an RBF SVM and read its bumps","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nz = np.array([-0.55, -0.35, -0.15, 0.05, 0.25, 0.5, -2.0, -1.55, -1.25, 1.2, 1.6, 2.05])\ny = np.array([-1] * 6 + [1] * 6)                       # -1 healthy, +1 failing\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"rbf\", gamma=1, C=10, tol=1e-9).fit(z.reshape(-1, 1), y)   # one feature, so one column\n\nprint(\"support vectors:\", svm.support_vectors_.ravel())\nprint(\"alpha*y        :\", np.round(svm.dual_coef_[0], 3))\nprint(\"offset b       :\", np.round(svm.intercept_, 4))","out":"support vectors: [-0.55  0.5  -1.25  1.2 ]\nalpha*y        : [-1.969 -1.969  1.969  1.969]\noffset b       : [0.3245]","walk":[["z.reshape(-1, 1)","scikit-learn needs a 2-D table with one row per reading and one column per feature. <code>reshape(-1, 1)</code> turns a flat list of 12 numbers into 12 rows and 1 column; <code>-1</code> means \"work out this size yourself\"."],["SVC(kernel=\"rbf\", gamma=1, C=10)","The RBF SVM. <code>gamma</code> is γ in exp(−γ·d²) and <code>C</code> caps α. A tight <code>tol</code> gives exact weights."],["svm.support_vectors_.ravel()","The positions of the bump centres. <code>support_vectors_</code> is a table (4 rows, 1 column); <code>ravel()</code> flattens it to a plain list."],["svm.dual_coef_[0]","α·y for each support vector, listed in the same order as above, but ordered by class (healthy first). Healthy supports are negative and failing supports positive: the up and down bumps."],["svm.intercept_","The offset b, one number per boundary (so an array of length 1)."]],"tip":"Four support vectors, four bumps: the same four the widget rings at the default settings."},{"title":"Rebuild the score at a probe from the votes","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nz = np.array([-0.55, -0.35, -0.15, 0.05, 0.25, 0.5, -2.0, -1.55, -1.25, 1.2, 1.6, 2.05])\ny = np.array([-1] * 6 + [1] * 6)\nsvm = SVC(kernel=\"rbf\", gamma=1, C=10, tol=1e-9).fit(z.reshape(-1, 1), y)\n\nprobe = 1.0\nsv, ay, b = svm.support_vectors_.ravel(), svm.dual_coef_[0], svm.intercept_[0]\nK = np.exp(-1 * (probe - sv) ** 2)                     # closeness of each support vector to the probe\nvotes = ay * K                                         # signed height times closeness\nprint(\"votes:\", np.round(votes, 4), \" sum:\", round(votes.sum(), 4))\nprint(\"score:\", round(votes.sum() + b, 4), \" sklearn:\", np.round(svm.decision_function([[probe]]), 4))\nprint(\"effective voters:\", round(K.sum() ** 2 / (K**2).sum(), 2))","out":"votes: [-0.1782 -1.5335  0.0125  1.8918]  sum: 0.1926\nscore: 0.5171  sklearn: [0.5171]\neffective voters: 2.19","walk":[["K = np.exp(-1 * (probe - sv) ** 2)","One K per support vector, all at once: <code>probe - sv</code> subtracts the probe from every centre, <code>** 2</code> squares each distance, and the <code>-1</code> is γ."],["votes = ay * K","Element-by-element product: each support vector's signed height times its closeness. These are the numbers in the widget's vote table."],["votes.sum() + b","The score is the sum of the votes plus the offset: the formula f(z) = Σ αᵢyᵢ K + b written as code."],["svm.decision_function([[probe]])","scikit-learn's own score at the probe. It matches the hand-built 0.5171, which proves you read <code>dual_coef_</code>, <code>support_vectors_</code> and <code>intercept_</code> correctly."],["K.sum() ** 2 / (K**2).sum()","Effective voters (ΣK)² / ΣK². Note <code>K**2</code> squares each entry before summing, while <code>K.sum() ** 2</code> squares the total."]],"tip":"If your rebuilt score differs from decision_function, you almost certainly mixed up the order of the arrays or used the wrong gamma in the exponent."},{"title":"Watch the votes die as gamma grows","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nz = np.array([-0.55, -0.35, -0.15, 0.05, 0.25, 0.5, -2.0, -1.55, -1.25, 1.2, 1.6, 2.05])\ny = np.array([-1] * 6 + [1] * 6)\nfor gamma in (0.1, 1, 10, 1000):\n    svm = SVC(kernel=\"rbf\", gamma=gamma, C=10, tol=1e-9).fit(z.reshape(-1, 1), y)\n    far = svm.decision_function([[3.0]])[0]            # a reading 0.95 beyond the last training reading\n    print(f\"gamma={gamma:<6} SVs={len(svm.support_):>2}  b={svm.intercept_[0]:.4f}  f(3.0)={far:.4f}\")","out":"gamma=0.1    SVs= 7  b=2.5901  f(3.0)=3.5475\ngamma=1      SVs= 4  b=0.3245  f(3.0)=0.3978\ngamma=10     SVs=11  b=0.2538  f(3.0)=0.2539\ngamma=1000   SVs=12  b=0.0000  f(3.0)=0.0000","walk":[["for gamma in (0.1, 1, 10, 1000)","Four bump widths: σ = 2.2, 0.71, 0.22 and 0.022."],["len(svm.support_)","The number of support vectors. It is not monotonic in γ: 7, then 4 at the sweet spot, then 11 and 12 as bumps become spikes."],["svm.decision_function([[3.0]])[0]","The score at z = 3.0. <code>[[3.0]]</code> is one row, one column; <code>[0]</code> takes the single number out of the result array."],["f\"{gamma:<6}\"","Formatting: left-align the value in a field 6 characters wide so the columns line up."]],"tip":"Compare f(3.0) with b in each row. For γ = 10 they agree to 4 digits: at that distance the votes are gone, and the offset is the whole score."}],"check":[{"q":"In an RBF SVM, what does the score f(z) approach as z moves far away from every support vector?","options":["Plus or minus infinity","Zero, whatever b is","The sum of all α·y","The offset b"],"answer":3,"why":"Every K = exp(−γd²) tends to 0, so every vote tends to 0 and only b is left. On these readings b = 0.32, and f(3) = 0.40 is already close to it."},{"q":"A support vector at z = 2 has α·y = −1.5. With γ = 2, what is its vote at the probe z* = 2.5?","options":["−1.50","−0.91","−0.55","−1.17"],"answer":1,"why":"d² = 0.25, so K = e^(−2 × 0.25) = e^(−0.5) = 0.6065 and the vote is −1.5 × 0.6065 = −0.91. The value −0.55 forgets to square the distance, −1.17 forgets γ, and −1.50 forgets K."},{"q":"You raise γ from 1 to 1000 (C stays 10) and put the probe at z* = 1.0. What does the vote table show?","options":["The same four votes as before","An empty table, because no reading is a support vector","Bigger votes, because the bumps are taller","Every vote is close to 0, effective voters is about 1, and the score is just b, which is about 0"],"answer":3,"why":"At γ = 1000 all 12 readings are support vectors, but the nearest one is 0.2 away, so K = e^(−1000 × 0.04) = e^(−40) ≈ 0. Every vote vanishes and f is just b, which is about 0 here."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend how an RBF SVM scores a new reading, and why the offset b decides when the reading is far from all the data.","model":"Each support vector adds a vote equal to its signed weight α·y times how close the reading is to it (exp(−γ × distance²)), and the sum of votes plus the offset b is the score. Far from every support vector all the closeness values are almost 0, so all the votes vanish and only b is left to decide."},"cheat":"RBF score: f(z) = Σ αᵢyᵢ exp(−γ(z − zᵢ)²) + b over the support vectors. Each term is a bump of height αᵢyᵢ (never above C in size) and width σ = 1/√(2γ). Sign of f is the class. Far from every support vector f ≈ b. Large γ: narrow spikes, about one voter, b decides the gaps."},"svm-kernels/c-gamma-grid":{"idea":"An RBF SVM has two dials. γ sets how far each point's influence reaches. C sets how hard the model insists on fitting every training point. Score a log grid of (C, γ) pairs on held-out data and choose from the good region.","why":"Too small a γ or C gives a boundary that is too smooth: 77% on held-out points in the starting data. Too large a γ memorises: 100% on the training points but only 57% on new ones. The best pairs sit in a band. Judging by training accuracy would pick a bad cell. A grid finds the band.","analogy":"Preparing for an exam. γ is how narrowly you study: only the exact past questions (huge γ) or whole chapters (small γ). C is how strictly you demand every practice question right. Studying only past papers and demanding 100% gives a perfect practice score and a poor real exam. You find a good mix with a mock exam of questions you did not practise on. The grid tries many mixes and marks each on that mock exam.","vocab":[["γ (gamma)","The reach of one point. Bump width is <span class=\"eq\">σ = 1/√(2γ)</span>. Large γ means narrow bumps and a boundary that can wrap around single points."],["C","The cap on each support vector's weight, and so the price of a mistake. Small C tolerates many errors. Large C fights for every training point."],["Grid search","Train one model for every (C, γ) pair on a grid and score each one. Here 6 × 6 = 36 models."],["Log scale","Steps of ×10 (0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1000) instead of steps of +1. C and γ act by multiplying, so equal ratios matter, not equal differences."],["Held-out accuracy","Accuracy on points the model never trained on. The honest measure of how it will do on new data."],["Underfit and overfit","Underfit: too smooth, training and held-out accuracy both low. Overfit: memorising, training accuracy high and held-out accuracy much lower."],["γ = \"scale\"","scikit-learn's default: <span class=\"eq\">1 / (n_features × Var(X))</span>, where Var(X) is the variance over every entry of the training data."],["Cross-validation","Split the training data into 5 parts, train on 4 and score on the 5th, rotate, and average. It scores a cell without touching the test set."]],"steps":[{"h":"Two dials, two jobs","p":"γ sets the width of every bump: σ = 1/√(2γ). A small γ gives wide, overlapping bumps and a smooth, almost straight boundary. A large γ gives narrow bumps that can wrap around single points. C caps how tall a bump can get, which is the price of a mistake. A small C allows many mistakes. A large C fights for every training point."},{"h":"Why a grid on a log scale","p":"No formula says which (C, γ) is best for your data, so you try pairs. Both dials act by multiplying, so useful steps are factors of 10 (0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100, 1000), not additions. Six values each give 6 × 6 = 36 cells, and every cell is a full SVM trained from scratch."},{"h":"Score each cell on points it did not train on","p":"Training accuracy only measures memory. The right-hand column of the grid has 100% training accuracy in every row, and about 57% on held-out points. So each cell is scored on 150 held-out points that the SVM never saw. Pick the cell by that number."},{"h":"The corners tell the story","p":"Bottom-left (C = 0.01, γ = 0.01): too smooth and too cheap, 79% on training and 77% held-out. That is underfit. Right-hand column (γ = 1000): each point gets its own island, every point is a support vector, 100% training and about 57% held-out. That is overfit. The good cells lie between."},{"h":"The good cells form a band, not a point","p":"At γ = 10 any C up to 10 gives 89%. At γ = 1 you need C = 10 for 91%. At γ = 0.1 you need C = 1000 to reach 89%. Each tenfold fall in γ needs C to rise 100 to 1000 times. A small γ makes the model stiff, and a big C pushes it to bend anyway. The best cells run along a diagonal."},{"h":"Where to start: γ = \"scale\"","p":"scikit-learn's default is 1 / (n_features × Var(X)). For these moons it is 0.86, almost exactly the γ = 1 column, which is a good column. So \"scale\" is a sensible centre for the search: keep γ within a factor of 100 of it on each side, and tune C on a log grid."},{"h":"Read near-ties honestly","p":"One test point is 1/150, about 0.7 percentage points. The standard error of an accuracy p measured on n points is √(p(1 − p)/n): for 90.7% on 150 points it is 2.4 points. Cells within that distance of the best are statistically tied (9 of the 36 here). Choose the simplest of them, not the single highest number."},{"h":"The honest procedure","p":"This page scores every cell on one held-out set and then picks the best by looking at those same scores, so its 91% is slightly optimistic. In your own work choose (C, γ) by 5-fold cross-validation on the training data only (36 cells × 5 folds = 180 fits), refit once, and use the test set a single time at the end."}],"example":{"title":"reading the grid: five cells and one decision","setup":"Moons with noise 0.25, the starting data: 150 training points and 150 held-out points. Five cells of the grid, with the number of correct predictions out of 150.","table":{"head":["Cell (C, γ)","Train correct","Test correct","Test accuracy","Gap (train − test)","Support vectors","Reading"],"rows":[["(0.01, 0.01)","118","115","76.7%","2.0 points","150","underfit: too smooth, too cheap"],["(1000, 0.1)","140","133","88.7%","4.7 points","38","good: on the band"],["(10, 1)","140","136","90.7%","2.7 points","35","the best cell"],["(10, 100)","149","128","85.3%","14.0 points","144","starting to memorise"],["(10, 1000)","150","86","57.3%","42.7 points","150","memorised"]]},"walk":["Accuracy is correct ÷ 150. The best cell, (C = 10, γ = 1), gets 136 of 150 held-out points right: 136 / 150 = 0.9067, or <b>90.7%</b>. On the training points it gets 140 / 150 = 93.3%.","Gap = training accuracy − test accuracy. For the best cell, 93.3 − 90.7 = 2.7 points: small. For (10, 1000), 100 − 57.3 = <b>42.7 points</b>: the model memorised. A gap above about 10 points is the warning sign.","One test point is worth 1/150 = 0.67 points. The standard error of the best cell's accuracy is √(0.907 × 0.093 / 150) = √0.000564 = 0.0237, about <b>2.4 points</b>.","The cell (1000, 0.1) gets 133 of 150 = 88.7%, which is 2.0 points below the best. That is less than one standard error, so the two cells are tied. So are 7 others: 9 of the 36 cells lie within 2.4 points of the best.","Among tied cells prefer the simpler model. (10, 1) uses 35 support vectors and (1000, 0.1) uses 38. A model with fewer support vectors is cheaper to store and to evaluate.","Cost of the search: 36 cells × 5 folds = 180 fits, plus one final fit. That is why you search a coarse log grid first and refine around the best region afterwards."],"result":"The best cell (C = 10, γ = 1) gets 136/150 = 90.7%, give or take 2.4 points. Nine cells are statistically tied and they form the diagonal band. The five cells show the pattern: too smooth (77%), good (89 to 91%), starting to memorise (85%) and memorised (57%)."},"read":[["Colour and number of a cell","Brighter means a higher value of the chosen metric. The number is that value in percent."],["Horizontal axis: γ (log scale)","Each column is ten times the one before it, from 0.01 on the left to 1000 on the right."],["Vertical axis: C (log scale)","Each row is ten times the one below it: C = 0.01 at the bottom, 1000 at the top."],["Dashed vertical line: γ = “scale”","scikit-learn's default γ for this data. It falls between columns when its value is not a power of 10."],["Thick dark frame","The selected cell. Click any cell to select it. At the start the best cell is selected."],["Grid shows: Test accuracy, Training accuracy, SV share","Accuracy on the 150 held-out points, accuracy on the 150 training points, or support vectors divided by 150."],["Bottom plot: Decision boundary of the selected cell","The tint is the predicted class. The solid line is f = 0 and the dashed lines are f = ±1. The dots are the 150 training points."],["Readout: C, γ","The values of the selected cell."],["Readouts: Training accuracy and Test accuracy","The two accuracies of the selected cell. Test turns green when the cell ties the best on the grid."],["Readout: Support vectors","Out of 150. A value of 150 means every training point is a support vector."],["Readout: Best cell on the grid (picked by test score)","The cell with the highest test accuracy. Ties go to the smaller C, then the smaller γ. Because the same scores pick it and report it, its test accuracy is a little optimistic; the page says so under the readouts."],["Readout: γ = “scale” for this data","1 / (2 × Var(X)) for the 150 training points (two features)."]],"experiments":[{"do":"Look at the cell selected at the start (C = 10, γ = 1, the best one). Then click a cell in the right-hand column, such as C = 10, γ = 1000.","see":"The first boundary is a smooth curve through the gap between the moons (35 support vectors, training 93%, test 91%). The second is a scatter of tiny islands around single points (150 of 150 support vectors, training 100%, test 57%).","why":"At γ = 1000 the bump width is σ = 0.022, far smaller than the distance between points. Each point gets its own island and the rest of the plane is decided by the offset b."},{"do":"Set <i>Grid shows</i> to <i>Training accuracy</i>.","see":"The whole γ = 1000 column shows 100% and the γ = 100 column shows 98 to 100%. The best test cell shows only 93%, and the bottom-left corner stays at 79%.","why":"Training accuracy rewards memory. The cell that fits the training points best is not the one that predicts new points best. Choosing by training accuracy would pick a memorising cell."},{"do":"Set <i>Grid shows</i> to <i>SV share</i>.","see":"The bottom row (C = 0.01) and the right-hand column are 100%. The lowest values, 21% to 25%, are in the γ = 1 column (C = 10 to 1000) and at C = 1000, γ = 0.1.","why":"With a tiny C every point must keep some weight, so all become support vectors. With a huge γ every point stands alone. In between only the points near the boundary matter, which is a sign of a comfortable fit."},{"do":"On the starting data (before pressing <i>New data</i>) move <i>Noise</i> to 0.05, then 0.25, 0.35 and 0.5, and count the cells that show 90 or more with <i>Test accuracy</i>.","see":"24 of 36 cells reach 90% at noise 0.05, 3 at 0.25, and none at 0.35 or 0.5. The best cell falls from 100% to 91%, 83% and 75%.","why":"Noise makes the two moons overlap, so even the ideal model makes mistakes. The ceiling drops, and fewer cells get close to 90%."},{"do":"Starting from the initial data (noise 0.25, Moons), press <i>New data</i> five times, noting the best cell each time.","see":"The best cell always has γ = 1 or 10, but its C is 0.01, 1000, 10, 100 and 1 on the five new samples. The best test accuracy runs from 94% to 97%.","why":"The best cell is the maximum of 36 noisy numbers measured on 150 points, each uncertain by about 2 points, so the exact winner is partly luck. The stable thing is the region, not the cell."},{"do":"Switch <i>Shape</i> to <i>Rings</i>.","see":"γ = “scale” is 1.53 here and the nearest column is γ = 1, which scores 95 to 98% in every row. The best cell is C = 100, γ = 0.1 at 99%.","why":"When the scale line sits on a column that is already good, you can start there and mostly tune C. On the moons the same column ranges from 81% at C = 0.01 to 91% at C = 10, so C mattered more there."}],"remember":[{"tag":"Picture","text":"A map of 36 cells. Bottom-left is too smooth, the right-hand column is memorised, and a diagonal ridge of good cells runs between them."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"Smaller γ needs larger C: here γ ÷ 10 needed C × 100 or more. Search along the diagonal, not across it."},{"tag":"Formula in words","text":"γ sets bump width σ = 1/√(2γ). C caps bump height. Width and height together set how flexible the boundary is."},{"tag":"Sanity check","text":"Always read train minus test. A gap above about 10 points means memorising (γ or C too big). Both low and close together means too smooth."},{"tag":"Trick","text":"Start with γ = \"scale\" and C = 1. Search C over 0.01 … 1000 and γ over scale/100 … scale × 100, then refine around the best region."},{"tag":"Rule of thumb","text":"A difference smaller than √(p(1 − p)/n) is noise: 2.4 points when 150 test points give about 90%."},{"tag":"Link to code","text":"<code>GridSearchCV(SVC(), {\"C\": [...], \"gamma\": [...]}, cv=5)</code> runs the whole grid. <code>cv_results_[\"mean_test_score\"].reshape(6, 6)</code> gives the table in the same layout as this page."}],"mistakes":[{"wrong":"\"The best cell is the one with the highest training accuracy.\"","right":"Training accuracy is 100% in the whole γ = 1000 column, where held-out accuracy is about 57%. Choose by held-out data."},{"wrong":"\"C and γ do the same job, so I only need to tune one.\"","right":"γ sets the width and C the height. At γ = 0.01 even C = 1000 reaches only 81%. At γ = 1, C moves the score from 81% to 91%. They interact, so tune them together."},{"wrong":"\"The cell with the highest number is the true best setting.\"","right":"On 150 test points the standard error is 2.4 points, and nine cells are within it. Choose the middle of the plateau, or the simplest tied cell."},{"wrong":"\"It is fine to pick the cell by the test score.\"","right":"This page does it for teaching. In real work use cross-validation on the training data and look at the test set once, or the reported accuracy will be a little too good."}],"code":[{"title":"Run a log grid with cross-validation","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import make_moons\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV, train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX, y = make_moons(n_samples=300, noise=0.25, random_state=12)\nXtr, Xte, ytr, yte = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.5, stratify=y, random_state=0)\n\npowers = [10.0**k for k in range(-2, 4)]                  # 0.01, 0.1, ..., 1000: a log grid\ngrid = GridSearchCV(SVC(kernel=\"rbf\"), {\"C\": powers, \"gamma\": powers}, cv=5).fit(Xtr, ytr)\ntable = grid.cv_results_[\"mean_test_score\"].reshape(6, 6)  # rows follow C, columns follow gamma\nprint((100 * table).round().astype(int))\nprint(\"best:\", grid.best_params_, \" CV:\", round(grid.best_score_, 3), \" test:\", round(grid.score(Xte, yte), 3))","out":"[[77 78 87 90 79 53]\n [77 79 91 90 79 53]\n [79 85 93 93 78 55]\n [85 90 90 89 79 55]\n [85 88 89 85 79 54]\n [87 91 90 83 77 54]]\nbest: {'C': 1.0, 'gamma': 10.0}  CV: 0.927  test: 0.953","walk":[["test_size=0.5, stratify=y","Half the points for training, half held back. <code>stratify=y</code> keeps the share of each class equal in both halves."],["[10.0**k for k in range(-2, 4)]","A list comprehension: for k = −2, −1, 0, 1, 2, 3 it builds 10<sup>k</sup>, giving 0.01 … 1000. <code>**</code> is \"to the power of\"."],["GridSearchCV(SVC(kernel=\"rbf\"), {\"C\": powers, \"gamma\": powers}, cv=5)","Tries all 36 pairs. Each one is scored by 5-fold cross-validation on the training half only, so the held-out half stays untouched."],[".cv_results_[\"mean_test_score\"].reshape(6, 6)","The mean CV accuracy of each cell as a flat list of 36 numbers, which <code>reshape(6, 6)</code> folds into rows (C, first parameter in alphabetical order) and columns (gamma)."],["grid.score(Xte, yte)","After the search, the grid refits the best cell on the whole training half. This one call is the single look at the test half."]],"tip":"Compare with the widget: the same diagonal ridge, and an almost-chance column (53 to 55%) at γ = 1000."},{"title":"Where does gamma = \"scale\" land?","py":"import numpy as np\nfrom sklearn.datasets import make_moons\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score\nfrom sklearn.svm import SVC\n\nX, y = make_moons(n_samples=300, noise=0.25, random_state=12)\ng = 1 / (X.shape[1] * X.var())                   # 1 / (features x variance of every entry of X)\nprint(\"gamma 'scale' =\", round(g, 3))\nsame = np.allclose(SVC(gamma=\"scale\").fit(X, y).decision_function(X), SVC(gamma=g).fit(X, y).decision_function(X))\nprint(\"same model as gamma='scale':\", same)\nfor name, gamma in [(\"scale\", \"scale\"), (\"scale / 100\", g / 100), (\"scale x 100\", g * 100)]:\n    print(f\"{name:<12} CV = {cross_val_score(SVC(C=1, gamma=gamma), X, y, cv=5).mean():.3f}\")","out":"gamma 'scale' = 0.918\nsame model as gamma='scale': True\nscale        CV = 0.933\nscale / 100  CV = 0.817\nscale x 100  CV = 0.887","walk":[["X.shape[1] * X.var()","<code>X.shape[1]</code> is the number of features (2). <code>X.var()</code> is the variance of all entries together, not per column. Their product is what gets inverted."],["np.allclose(a, b)","True when two arrays agree to within rounding. Here it proves that <code>gamma=\"scale\"</code> is exactly this formula."],["gamma=g / 100","A γ one hundred times smaller: bumps ten times wider, boundary almost straight."],["cross_val_score(..., cv=5).mean()","Trains 5 times, each time scoring on the fifth that was held out, and averages the 5 accuracies."]],"tip":"The default is already near the best of the three. A range of ×100 on each side is the usual width for a first grid."}],"check":[{"q":"A cell shows 100% training accuracy, 57% held-out accuracy and 150 of 150 support vectors. What does it tell you?","options":["The held-out set is too small to say anything","γ is far too large: the model memorised the training points","No kernel could separate these data","C is far too small: the model is too smooth"],"answer":1,"why":"Training near 100% with held-out near chance and every point a support vector is the memorising pattern: each point has its own tiny island (σ is much smaller than the point spacing)."},{"q":"The best cell gets 136 of 150 held-out points right. A neighbouring cell gets 133. Is the best cell really better? (The standard error of 90.7% on 150 points is about 2.4 points.)","options":["No: the gap is 2.0 points, which is less than one standard error","Yes: any higher count means a better model","Yes: 3 more correct out of 150 is a big difference","No: because both are above 85%"],"answer":0,"why":"The difference is 3 points out of 150, that is 3/150 = 2.0 percentage points. One standard error is 2.4 points, so the gap is within noise. Prefer the simpler of the two."},{"q":"You pick the cell with the highest <i>training</i> accuracy instead of the highest test accuracy. What held-out accuracy do you get?","options":["About 77%, the underfit corner","About 91%, the same as the best cell","Between 56% and 85%: the cells with 100% training accuracy are in the γ = 100 and γ = 1000 columns","Above 95%, since training accuracy is a stricter test"],"answer":2,"why":"Only cells with large γ reach 100% training accuracy: C = 100 or 1000 at γ = 100 (85% held-out) and every cell at γ = 1000 (56 to 57%). The underfit corner has the lowest training accuracy, so it would never be chosen."}],"teachback":{"prompt":"In two sentences, explain to a friend what γ and C each control in an RBF SVM and why you tune them on a log grid scored on held-out data.","model":"γ sets how far each point's influence reaches (large γ means narrow bumps that can memorise single points) and C caps how much the model will pay for mistakes; they trade off, so the good settings form a diagonal band. You search powers of ten because both act by multiplying, and you score each pair on points it never trained on, because training accuracy reaches 100% for a memorising model."},"cheat":"Tune C and γ together on log grids (0.01 … 1000), scoring each cell on held-out data or by cross-validation, never on training accuracy. γ sets bump width σ = 1/√(2γ), C caps bump height. Good cells form a diagonal: smaller γ needs larger C. Start from γ = \"scale\" = 1/(n_features·Var(X)). Differences under one standard error are noise."}});
